# Strategies for Improving the Functionality of Zeolitic Imidazolate Frameworks: Tailoring Nanoarchitectures for Functional Applications

## abstract
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), a subclass of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) built with tetrahedral metal ions and imidazolates, offer permanent porosity and high thermal and chemical stabilities. While ZIFs possess some attractive physical and chemical properties, it remains important to enhance their functionality for practical application. Here, an overview of the extensive strategies which have been developed to improve the functionality of ZIFs is provided, including linker modifications, functional hybridization of ZIFs via the encapsulation of guest species (such as metal and metal oxide nanoparticles and biomolecules) into ZIFs, and hybridization with polymeric matrices to form mixed matrix membranes for industrial gas and liquid separations. Furthermore, the developed strategies for achieving size and shape control of ZIF nanocrystals are considered, which are important for optimizing the textural characteristics as well as the functional performance of ZIFs and their derived materials/hybrids. Moreover, the recent trends of using ZIFs as templates for the derivation of nanoporous hybrid materials, including carbon/metal, carbon/oxide, carbon/sulfide, and carbon/phosphide hybrids, are discussed. Finally, some perspectives on the potential future research directions and applications for ZIFs and ZIF-derived materials are offered.

Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), a subclass of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) built with tetrahedral metal ions and imidazolates, offer permanent porosity and high thermal and chemical stabilities. While ZIFs possess some attractive physical and chemical properties, it remains important to enhance their functionality for practical application. Here, an overview of the extensive strategies which have been developed to improve the functionality of ZIFs is provided, including linker modifications, functional hybridization of ZIFs via the encapsulation of guest species (such as metal and metal oxide nanoparticles and biomolecules) into ZIFs, and hybridization with polymeric matrices to form mixed matrix membranes for industrial gas and liquid separations. Furthermore, the developed strategies for achieving size and shape control of ZIF nanocrystals are considered, which are important for optimizing the textural characteristics as well as the functional performance of ZIFs and their derived materials/hybrids. Moreover, the recent trends of using ZIFs as templates for the derivation of nanoporous hybrid materials, including carbon/metal, carbon/oxide, carbon/sulfide, and carbon/ phosphide hybrids, are discussed. Finally, some perspectives on the potential future research directions and applications for ZIFs and ZIF-derived materials are offered.
## 1. Introduction
Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIF) as a subclass of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted large scientific interests due to their high porosity, excellent mechanical stability, tunable surface properties, and their exceptional chemical (high resistance to alkaline water and organic solvents due to the strong metalnitrogen bonds) and thermal stabilities.[1] The combination of these features distinguishes ZIFs from many other MOFs.[2] Structurally, ZIFs are grown from $\mathrm{{\calM}(I m i)_{4}}$ ( $\operatorname{Imi}{}=$ imidazolate) tetrahedra constructed by the coordination between $\mathrm{M}^{2+}$ cations and Imi anions, where Imi units constitute the bridges for connecting the metal centers.[3] The metal ions mimic the role of silicon and the imidazolate anions form bridges that resemble the role of oxygen in zeolites. The bonding angle formed by imidazolates when bridging transition metals $(\mathrm{M}{-}\mathrm{Im}{-}\mathrm{M})$ is close to the $_{\mathrm{Si-O-Si}}$ bond angle $(145^{\circ})$ in zeolites, which gives rise to many different ZIFs with zeolite-like topological structures.[4] The metal centers are typically made of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ (ZIF-8) or ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ (ZIF-67), however, they can be substituted with other metal ions such as $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ and so on.[5]
The synthesis of ZIFs essentially depends on the coordination ability of the metal centers and organic linkers, which offers a greater flexibility and controllability than conventional zeolites. The pore size and the adsorption properties of ZIFs can be tailored by changing or chemically modifying the anionic imidazolate linker. As of today, more than 90 ZIF structures have been reported, some of which share the same topology as zeolites, while others exhibit crystal structures which are different from zeolites.[6] The representative ZIFs with zeolitic structures are shown in Figure 1. Their intrinsic porous characteristics, abundant functionality as well as exceptional thermal and chemical stabilities of ZIFs have led to their utilization in a broad range of applications including gas capture and separation, sensing, catalysis, electronic devices and drug delivery.[4] Although, pure ZIFs possess some good inherent physical and chemical properties, it is still highly desirable to modify them further to truly unlock their potential for industrial applications.
To date, many methods have been developed to enhance the functionality of ZIFs, such as:
i)	 linker functionalization (either by post-synthetic modification (PSM) or solvent-assisted ligand exchange (SALE) (also termed as post-synthetic exchange (PSE));   
ii)	 encapsulation of metal and metal oxide nanoparticles (NPs), and   
iii)	encapsulation of biological molecules.
The framework modifications of ZIFs allow for the tuning of their physical and chemical properties to increase the functional performance, particularly in adsorption, separation and sensors. A number of methods have been utilized to modify the surface properties of ZIFs to improve their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ affinity and separation properties, including PSM and PSE of the linker and metal center. PSM involves the reaction of a small molecule with a functional group on the organic linker of the ZIF, whereas PSE involves the heating the ZIF in a solution containing a different linker or metal ion that exchanges into the ZIF material, while still maintaining the original topology or crystal structure of the parent ZIF.[7,8]
Despite exhibiting unique structural features, most of the applications of ZIFs are still restricted to microporous regime.[9] This limits the diffusion of chemical species and their interactions with active sites in ZIFs. The fabrication of mesoporous structures for silica, zeolites and carbons have been widely demonstrated; however, it is much more challenging to create mesoporous ZIFs.[10–12] Hence, it is worthwhile to focus on developing strategies to enhance their molecular diffusion, adsorption, catalytic, electronic, and electrical properties, such as by encapsulating metal NPs, biological molecules and other functional materials. The tailorable pore size of ZIFs can enable the engineering of their bulk and surface structures by introducing a variety of foreign NPs or small molecules that are capable of imparting novel functionality into them. In the past few years, there has been an extensive interest in the incorporation of biological molecules such as
