The control of the size and morphology of ZIFs is important for optimizing the textural properties and functional performance of ZIFs and their derived materials. The size control of ZIF particles is often accomplished by modifying the synthesis parameters such as the choice of solvent, the ratio of zinc ions to 2-methylimid­azole (2-MeIm), the reactivity of the metal salts, but can also be achieved by adding surfactants or more recently, block copolymers (BCPs).[4,19] In comparison, shape-controlled
ZIF nanocrystals (NCs) have been obtained through both non-templated and templateassisted approach. The template-free method relies on the modifications of one or more parameters during the synthesis of the ZIF NCs, whereas the template-assisted method typically relies on the use of soft templates (e.g., surfactants and BCPs) or hard templates such as polystyrene (PS) spheres.[19,20] In general, the template-assisted method is far more versatile for creating a diverse morphology of ZIF NCs from 1D to 3D. Despite some early success, the morphology control of ZIF through template-assisted approaches needs to be further explored as different morphologies may lead to different exposed facts, which in turn may influence the functional performance of ZIF materials and their derived products.
Mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) are typically made up of an inorganic/organic hybrid in the form of macro- or NPs (discrete phase) incorporated into a continuous polymeric matrix.[21] They are most commonly employed in gas separation and capture. The utilization of two distinguished materials with different flux and selectivity (e.g., ZIFs and polymers) may provide a better opportunity to develop a more effective and selective gas separation membrane, by combining the easy processability of the polymers and the superior gas separation performance of the inorganic materials (e.g., ZIFs).[21] In recent years, ZIFs have emerged as highly versatile templates or precursors for the derivation of highly porous nanomaterials including carbons, oxides, sulfides, phosphides, and their hybrids, due to the presence of metal centers and organic linkers in ZIFs.[22] The derived nanostructures/hybrids typically inherit the original morphology, along with the large surface area and pore volume exhibited by the parent ZIF, which highlights the advantages of this emerging method.
Here, we will provide an overview of the various strategies which have been developed for enhancing the functionality of ZIFs, with focus on four main topics, including:
i)	 Structural modification of ZIFs and encapsulation of various guest species (such as metal and metal oxide NPs), bimetallic alloy NCs, biomolecules (e.g., enzymes and drug molecules)) in ZIFs.   
ii)	 Size and shape control of ZIF NCs, which have a direct impact on the nanoporous materials/hybrids derived from ZIFs.   
iii)	Functional hybridization of ZIFs with polymers to form MMMs for industrial gas and liquid separations, and   
iv)	The use of ZIFs as templates for fabricating highly nano­ porous hybrid materials such as metal/carbon and metal oxide/carbon hybrids, and more recently, metal sulfides and phosphides and their composites with carbon.
## 2. Strategies for Improving the Functionality of ZIFs
## 2.1. Strategy I: Linker Functionalization
The presence of organic ligands in MOFs allows for the possibilities of introducing a wide variety of functional groups via direct synthesis or post-synthetic approaches, such as PSM,[23] postsynthetic deprotection (PSD)[24] and PSE.[8] Some of the most commonly used methods to improve the functionality of MOFs (including ZIFs) are linker modification and linker exchange. These methods are particularly attractive for imparting or enhancing the functional behavior, including catalysis, selective gas sorption, ionic conductivity, redox conductivity, and mediating site-isolated chemistry.[25] Linker exchange and functionalization are intrinsically linked with the presence of defects in MOFs. For instance, the presence of defects in MOFs have enabled linker substitution (as opposed to linker removal) to create multivariate MOFs – that is, frameworks encompassing several distinct functionalities by the incorporation of different chemical groups on their organic linkers through mixing functionalized linkers based on the same backbone.[26] According to Fischer et al.,[27] these defects can be categorized into: i) point defects (e.g., vacancies); ii) line defects (e.g., dislocations); iii) planar defects (e.g., stacking faults and boundaries), iv) micro- and mesoscale volume defects (e.g., inclusions and voids), and iv) macroscale volume defects (e.g., mesopores, cracks, inclusions). Depending on the locations, defects can be further specified into: i) external or surface defects and ii) internal defects, the latter of which may arise as a consequence of partially missing organic linkers or metal nodes. In the sections below, some case studies related to the PSM and PSE of ZIFs will be discussed.
## 2.1.1. Post-Synthetic Modification (PSM) of ZIFs
PSM can be defined as heterogeneous chemical reactions employed to achieve the functionalization of pre-synthesized MOF structures by:[25]
i)	 linker modification, including (a) covalent modification, deprotection of linker functionality (including demetalation), electron addition (reduction) and concomitant incorporation of charge compensating ions.   
ii)	 metal node modification (i.e., transmetalation), including incorporation of non-framework or pendant ligands via dative bonding to coordinatively unsaturated metal sites, alkyl or silyl grafting to oxygen atoms in metal-oxide nodes, and attachment of metal ions or complexes at node oxygen sites by atomic layer deposition (ALD) or by reaction with organometallic species in dry solutions.
In the case of ZIFs, PSM of the metal nodes/centers is difficult to achieve due to the lack of open metal sites in many ZIFs.[23] Therefore, PSM of the organic linker is the most commonly used method to functionalize ZIF materials.
PSM has been widely used to enhance the selectivity and catalytic activity of various ZIFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation, and so on. For instance, the Caro group has developed a novel covalent functionalization method to prepare reproducible ZIF-90 molecular sieve membranes by using 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane (APTES) as a covalent linker between the ZIF-90 layer and alumina $(\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3})$ support via imines condensation.[28] In this process, the ethoxy groups of the APTES initially reacted with surface hydroxyl groups of the $\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ support. Next, the amino groups reacted with the aldehyde groups in ZIF-90 by imines condensation, which was then followed by the nucleation and growth of the ZIF-90 crystals on the surface of the porous $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ supports. Finally, after the solvothermal reaction at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the surface of the APTES-modified support was completely covered with well intergrown rhombic dodecahedra and a compact ZIF-90 layer with a thickness of around $20\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . When evaluated for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation, the ZIF-90 membrane showed excellent permeance for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at both room and elevated temperatures, over $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gases as well as at an elevated partial pressure from 0.5 bar to 1.5 bar $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Additionally, the ZIF-90 membrane showed a good hydrothermal stability in the presence of steam and their high permeance for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as well as their high $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity were well maintained under such harsh conditions.
## 2.1.2. Post-Synthetic Exchange (PSE) or Solvent-Assisted Linker Exchange (SALE) of ZIFs
Another viable strategy for functionalizing ZIFs is through PSE (also often termed as “solvent-assisted linker exchange (SALE)”). SALE is commonly used to impart new properties into MOFs, such as by improving proton conductivity, introducing catalytically active moieties, enhancing photochemical $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ reduction, and so on.[25] By exchanging linkers in a relatively simple framework, one can introduce novel chemical functions in a manner that is controlled by the exchange kinetics. Furthermore, SALE can even be used to synthesize frameworks that were previously unobtainable by direct synthesis methods, as they would crystallize in another topology.[26] The materials obtained through SALE often display a broad variety of thermal and mechanical behavior and stability. In SALE, the template MOF (e.g., ZIF) is placed in an excess solution containing a secondary linker.[25] Following the SALE reaction, the daughter framework retains the inherent topology of the parent MOF, however, the linker from the reaction solution will have replaced the original linker of the MOF.[29] The SALE process is reversible as it is possible to firstly exchange a linker with a functionalized linker and subsequently, exchange the functionalized linker with the original linker.[25] The two main factors which influence the exchange rate of SALE are:
i)	 The size of the organic linkers. Smaller linkers can easily enter into the pores of the MOFs, allowing for the linker exchange to occur more easily. On the other hand, SALE with larger linkers is more challenging to perform, however some successful cases have been reported by the Hupp group[30] and the Rosi group.[31]   
ii)	 The size of the MOF crystals. Smaller crystal sizes enable a faster rate of ligand exchange compared to larger crystals due to the faster diffusion rate.[32]
iii)	The choice of solvent. The polarity and the coordinating ability of the solvent have been proposed to play a role in the SALE reaction.[33]
The Cohen group has introduced a general PSE concept to introduce a redox-active transition metal, specifically $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ , into “inert” ZIFs.[8] To accomplish this goal, activated ZIF-71 (with a rhombohedral (RHO) topology) was incubated in a solution containing $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}$ (acac $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ acetylacetonate) in methanol at $55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The X-ray fluorescence (XRF) analysis revealed that the exchanged ZIF-71 (termed as “ZIF-71 $(\mathrm{Zn/Mn})^{\dprime})$ ) had $12\%$ of the tetrahedral $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ centers exchanged for $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ which was further supported by the decrease in the total Zn content in the exchanged ZIF-71 from $20.2\%$ to $16.7\%$ . The highly porous RHO topology and crystallinity of the original ZIF-71 were well retained after PSE and the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of the exchanged ZIF-71 $(966~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ was very similar to that of the pristine ZIF-71 $(964\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . In terms of stability, the exchanged ZIF-71 $(Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Mn})$ displayed nearly identical thermal stability (up to ${\approx}400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and decomposition steps as pristine ZIF-71 (termed as $\mathrm{ZIF{-}71(\mathrm{Zn})}|$ based on the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) results. Interestingly, the developed method could also be applied to ZIF-8 which possesses a different linker (2-MeIm as opposed to 4,5-dichloro-imidazole) and a different topology (RHO for ZIF-8 and sodalite (SOD) for ZIF-71). Similar to the case of ZIF-71, $\approx10\%$ of the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ centers in ZIF-8 was exchanged by $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ and the BET surface area of the exchanged ZIF-8 (termed as “ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn/Mn})^{\dprime})$ was slightly higher $(1595~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ than that of the non-exchanged one (termed as “ZIF-8 $(\mathrm{Zn})^{\dprime})$ $(1531~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , suggesting that the metal cation exchange between $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ occurred, rather than the trapping of $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ in the pores, which would have otherwise decreased the specific surface area.
In the same study, Cohen and co-authors also demonstrated the possible PSE of the organic linkers of the two ZIFs. By incubating the activated ZIF-71 in a methanol solution of 4-bromoimidazole at $55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days, $35\%$ of the 4-bromoimidazole had been incorporated into the ZIF (replacing the initial 4,5-dichloroimidazole), as identified by nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR), thus, indicating the successful ligand exchange. Interestingly, the ligand exchanged ZIF-71 $(\mathrm{Cl}_{2}/\mathrm{Br})$ could be treated with $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}$ in methanol to promote the formation of a bimetallic and multifunctional $\mathrm{ZIF{\cdot}71(C l_{2}/B r){\cdot}(Z n/M n)}$ (termed "stepwise PSE, see Figure 2a). Moreover, after the stepwise PSE, the inherent RHO topology and crystallinity of the parent ZIF-71 were retained. Using the same stepwise PSE strategy, the 2-MeIm ligand of ZIF-8 could be exchanged with 2-ethylimidazole (2-EtIm) by dispersing the ZIF-8 in a methanolic solution of 2-EtIm, before being treated with $\operatorname{Mn}(\operatorname{acac})_{2}$ to achieve the cation exchange between $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ and $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ . Again, the original SOD topology of the ZIF-8 was well-maintained after the stepwise PSE, thereby, highlighting the advantages of the proposed stepwise PSE.
SALE has also been used to increase the hydrophobicity of the ZIF frameworks through linker exchange with hydrophobic linkers. The Hupp group has demonstrated the first example of selective SALE on a series of mixed linker ZIFs including ZIF-69, ZIF-76 and ZIF-78 in which only one of the linkers (i.e., the 2-benzimiadazolate-containing linkers) was replaced rather than both linkers.[29] ZIF-69 is composed of a 1:1 ratio of 2-nitroimidazolate (nim) and 5-chlorobenzimidazolate (cbim) linkers coordinated to tetrahedral zinc nodes. By submerging ZIF-69 in a concentrated $(2{\mathrm{~M}})$ solution of 5-(trifluoromethyl) benzimidazole (fbim) in $n$ -butanol at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days, ${\approx}95\%$ of cbim was replaced by fbim and the resulting material is termed as SALEM-10 (Figure 2b, c). The same SALE strategy could be applied to ZIF-76 and ZIF-78, and in both cases, ${\approx}90\%$ of cbim was replaced by fbim. The corresponding products are termed as SALEM-11 and SALEM-12, respectively. The contact angle measurements revealed that the SALEMs showed much larger contact angles compared to the parent ZIFs, indicating the increase in hydrophobicity.
SALE has been reported to drastically affect both the stability and the adsorption properties of the resulting frameworks.[34,35] For instance, the Sen group has recently investigated the SALE of ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 with 1,2,3-triazole and found that after the SALE reaction, the 2-methylimidazole ligand was completely substituted with 1,2,3-triazole, which resulted in the transformation of the topological transformation of the parent framework from an SOD network to a diamond (DIA) network.[35] The resulting MOFs, Co-triazolate (MET-4) and Zn-triazolate (MET-6) were stable up to $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , but they were less thermally stable than the parent ZIF-67 and ZIF-8, respectively. Furthermore, the BET surface areas of MET-4 and MET-6 were measured to be 710 and $720~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which were roughly only half of those of the parent ZIFs. However, despite the lower surface areas, their $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ uptake values at 1 bar and ambient (and near ambient) temperatures considerably exceed those of the parent compounds. This was attributed to the reduced pore size with additional nitrogen sites which enabled strong interactions between the linkers and methane. These examples highlight the potential of SALE as a powerful technique for incorporating structurally and chemically attractive linkers into ZIFs, particularly in situations where the desired linkers cannot be incorporated de novo.
## 2.2. Strategy II: Encapsulation of Metal and Metal Oxide Nanoparticles in ZIFs
Apart from linker functionalization, the functionality of ZIFs can also be enhanced by introducing guest species such as metal and metal oxide NPs. Guest species can either be embedded in the cavities of ZIFs or encapsulated by ZIFs. In the latter case, the guest species do not occupy the ZIF cavities, but instead are surrounded by the grown ZIF materials. NPs/ZIF composites can be prepared either by using ZIFs as templates to generate NPs within the cavities or by encapsulating the pre-synthesized NPs inside the ZIFs.[36] In the former case, small and naked NPs are typically embedded in the cavities, however, in the case of encapsulation, usually pre-synthesized NPs are stabilized with surfactants or capping agents and hydrodynamic radius of the NPs is much larger than the cavity size of the ZIFs. Very recently, another approach by directly adding metal ions in the synthetic systems has been reported.[37] After encapsulation of the metal ions inside the ZIFs, a reduction process is carried out so that metal ions would be reduced to metal NPs that stay inside the ZIFs.
The resulting composites arising from the encapsulation of NPs in ZIFs are frequently applied in heterogeneous catalysis and gas-storage applications. However, for these composites to be used effectively in these applications, proper control over the particle size, shape and surface charges is necessary.[38–40] Firstly, in order to fully explore the well-defined pore networks of ZIFs, the incorporated nanoparticle needs to be completely confined within the frameworks. Secondly, the decisive factor is the ability to control the size, shape and composition of the incorporated NPs such that the relevant properties of the particle can be systematically tuned. Thirdly, preserving the optical and catalytic properties of the metal NPs whilst preventing agglomeration is important, as agglomeration is often encountered in many existing encapsulation strategies.[41–43] Finally, achieving a satisfactory control over the spatial distribution of one or more type of NPs within the ZIF matrix is also crucial.
In general, the encapsulation of guest species into ZIFs can be achieved via two fundamental strategies:[41]
i)	 the encapsulation of a species inside the ZIF cavity in which case the cavity size of the ZIF has to be matched with the cavity size of the guest species, and   
ii)	 the assembly of the ZIF nanostructures via hetero-nucleation around the pre-formed unprotected guest species such as highly disperse Pt NPs within the nanostructures of ZIF-8.
Each of these strategies has their own advantages and disadvantages. Based on certain selected case studies and critical analysis of the growth technique of ZIFs to embed guest inorganic NPs, we will attempt to reveal the significant features that enable the successful creation of such unique composite materials while preserving the original properties of the guest species for a wide range of applications.
## 2.2.1. Encapsulation of Metal Oxide Nanoparticles in ZIFs
ZIFs have been used as hosts for the encapsulation of metal oxide NPs to form ZIF $@$ metal oxide core–shell heterostructures. This type of encapsulation is advantageous for imparting the physical and chemical properties exhibited by the oxide constituent into the ZIF material. At the same time, the encapsulation of the metal oxide NPs by the ZIF particles can provide them protection from heat and harsh chemical environment, whilst also preventing aggregation of the metal oxide NPs.[44] The encapsulation of nanosized semiconductors such as $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ within a ZIF shell is also possible upon adapting a suitable self-templated strategy, as schematically depicted in Figure 3a. In the formation of $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{nO}@ Ḍ \mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ free-standing nanorod and nanotube arrays, the core $\mathrm{znO}$ nanorods and nanotubes not only act as templates, but also as sources of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ for the formation of ZIF-8 (Figure 3b–e), with uniform distribution of Zn, N, and C elements (Figure 3f–i). These well-defined $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{nO}@ Ḍ \mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ nanorod and nanotube arrays have shown distinct photochemical responses which are different from pure $\mathrm{{znO}}$ , however, the functional performance is strongly governed by the limitations of aperture of the ZIFshell (Figure 3j).[45] The creation of such $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{nO@\bigcirc\mathrm{ZIF}}{\cdot}8$ core–shell structures demonstrates the possibilities of fabricating a wide range of heterostructures by adapting similar synthetic strategies to impart photochemical response based sensory features of the semiconductor component onto the ZIFs.
Tin oxide quantum dots $(\mathrm{QDs})\ @\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ ( $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ QDs $@$ ZIF-8) core–shell heterostructures have previously been synthesized via a simple in situ epoxide precipitation method.[46] In this method, the ZIF-8 NCs were initially immersed into a mixture solution containing chloride solution and propylene oxide, before being oxidation through the addition of hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ to generate the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\mathrm{QDs@ZIF{-}}8$ core–shell composites. Following encapsulation by ZIF-8, the size and morphology of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ QDs remained similar, highlighting the benefit of the proposed strategy. The combination of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ QDs with ZIF-8 could maximize the utilization of active material which resulted in a maximum specific capacitance of up to $931\ \mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a scan rate of $5\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ , when employed as a supercapacitor anode. This value is considerably is higher than that of $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ QDs $(241\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}})$ or ZIF-8 $(99\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1})$ .
Chandra et al. have successfully encapsulated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs inside ZIFs to create $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ composites by reacting commercially available $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs with zinc nitrate and 2-MeIm for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature.[44] The specific surface area of the resulting $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}.8$ composites $(254~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ was significantly smaller than that of pure ZIF-8 $(1433\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , suggesting the reduction of the accessible void space for $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas molecules by incorporation of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs into the pores of ZIF-8. In terms of electronic properties, a slight lowering in the band-gap from $3.35\mathrm{eV}$ to $3.25\mathrm{eV}$ was observed for the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}.8$ composite compared to pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs due to the definite shift of microenvironment of the NPs within the framework. This decrease of the band-gap energy resulted in an increase of the visible-light photocatalytic activity of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ composite compared to pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs. Another potential reason for the enhancement in the photocatalytic activity of the composite is the presence of pores in the ZIF-8 crystals which were sufficiently large to provide access for methylene blue and rhodamine-B molecules to the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ NPs encapsulated inside the ZIF-8 NCs, thus, causing no hindrance to the catalytic degradation process.
ZIFs are known to exhibit relatively weak mechanical strengths and to enhance their functional applications, it is necessary to fabricate a ‘hard’ composite comprised of ZIF and a stronger secondary phase with a larger porosity, which may enable ionic or molecular species to travel in and out of ZIFs, without altering their pristine physicochemical properties. The mechanical properties (i.e., relative hardness and toughness) of ZIF-8 nanocubes could be significantly enhanced by reinforcing them with $m\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ shells.[47] To create the ZIF- $8@m{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ composite, the gel-like $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was initially deposited on the ZIF-8 core by the hydrolysis of tetraethyl orthosilicate (TEOS), in the presence of the surfactant cetyltrimethylammonium chloride (CTAC). Following calcination in nitrogen $(\Nu_{2})$ atmosphere, CTAC was removed and the gel-like $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ was converted to $m\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ . Interestingly, the gap existing between the ZIF-8 core and $m\mathrm{{SiO}}_{2}$ shell could be filled up through the regrowth of ZIF-8, whereby both $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ions and 2-MeIm linkers diffused through the $m\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ channels and were eventually deposited on the ZIF-8 cores. If the regrowth process of ZIF-8 is allowed to proceed for a long time, a ternary $m{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}@{\mathrm{ZIF}}\cdot8@m{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}$ composite is generated. The trend in the relative hardness and toughness was determined to be in the order of: $m{\mathrm{SiO}}_{2}@{\mathrm{ZIF}}$ - $8@m\mathrm{{SiO}}_{2}>\mathrm{{ZIF}}\mathrm{{-}}8@m\mathrm{{SiO}}_{2}>\mathrm{{ZIF}}\mathrm{{-}}8$ . The $\mathsf{Z I F{-}}8@m\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ composite exhibited better mechanical properties than ZIF-8 due to the strong $\mathrm{Si-O}$ bond in $m\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , and the ternary composite showed the best mechanical properties due to the close contact of $m\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ with ZIF-8 phase in the ZIF- $3@$ $m\mathrm{SiO}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ composite. However, the influence of the of $m\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ shell in preserving the microporosity and other pristine physicochemical properties of ZIF cores is still not well-understood at present.
## 2.2.2. Encapsulation of Noble Metal Nanoparticles in ZIFs
In recent years, MOFs have been employed as unique host matrices for encapsulating metal NPs to form metal $\mathsf{N P s@M O F}$ hybrids. The fabrication of such hybrids are advantageous as:[48–50]
i)	 They may exhibit novel physical and chemical properties due to synergistic effects between the encapsulated metal NPs (unique optical properties, electrical, magnetic and catalytic properties) and the MOF shells (structural adaptivity and flexi­ bility, ordered crystalline pores, and multi-coordination sites);   
ii)	 MOFs provide a molecular sieving effect and therefore, provide a high selectivity in catalytic reactions due to their defined pore apertures, and   
iii)	MOFs provide a quantum confinement effect which prevents the migration or aggregation of the core metal NPs, hence allowing for preservation of their chemical activity and stability.
Among the various MOFs, ZIFs have been considered as attractive host matrices for metal NPs due to their high chemical and thermal stabilities and large surface areas $(1000{-}2000~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .[51,52] To date, ZIFs have been successfully used to encapsulate individual noble metal NPs such as Au,[40,53,54] Pd,[52] Pt,[41] Ag,[55] Rh,[51] etc. or bimetallic alloys composed of two different metal NPs such as AuAg, PtPd,[48] AgPd,[56] etc. These metal ${\tt N P s@Z I F}$ composites have been employed in catalysis for various organic reactions such as hydrogenation,[52] dehydrogenation,[56] hydroformylation,[51] oxidative degradation,[48] oxidation[40,53,57] as well as in electrochemical sensing,[58] gas storage[49] and so on.
Based on the nanoparticle location and size, the composites of metal NPs and ZIFs can be divided into four different classes, as depicted in Figure 4.[36,53] Class A refers to the preferential accumulation of metal NPs at the external surface of the ZIF crystal and is often termed “NP/ZIFs”. These composites are produced when the size of the metal NPs substantially exceeds the size of the ZIF pore aperture. Class B describes the deposition of metal NPs throughout the bulk of the ZIF structure with the size of the metal NPs being close or slightly larger than the pore size of the ZIF matrix. In Class B, the formation of the metal NPs may be accompanied by a partial or full decomposition of the framework. due to the rapid agglomeration of the initially small nuclei in the case of weakly interacting frameworks. Class $C$ refers to the homogeneous distribution of cavity-size matching metal NPs throughout the ZIF matrix, which is most difficult to achieve.
In recent times, another possible classification, Class D has been added, which refers to the addition of surfactant-capped metal NPs with a larger size than the ZIF pore in a templated synthesis approach.[36,41] However, with regard to this class, often the resulting composites exhibit a lower loading amount of metal NPs compared to those produced in the other cases.
To date, there are two main methods which have been used to encapsulate metal NPs in ZIFs:[36,59]
i)	 Using ZIFs as templates, small and bare metal NPs or clusters are generated and embedded in the cavities of ZIFs, and   
ii)	 Pre-synthesized metal NPs stabilized by certain surfactants, capping agents or ions are encapsulated within the ZIF layers, and the hydrodynamic radius is usually much larger than the cavity size of the MOF and the NPs do not occupy the cavities but instead are surrounded by grown ZIF materials.
Method I: Encapsulation of Metal NPs by Cavity Size Matching: The encapsulation of metal NPs by the selective formation and stabilization of very small, bare metal NPs inside the cavities of ZIFs, while simultaneously achieving an even distribution of the NPs throughout the crystalline ZIF host matrix is a difficult task. To ensure success by this method, it is imperative to consider the overall robustness of the framework and structure, so as to avoid local damage or defect to the crystalline ZIF matrix during the encapsulation of metal NPs into their cavities. To date, reports on the encapsulation of metal NPs inside ZIFs by means of cavity-size matching are limited. An important breakthrough in this area was initially reported by the Fischer group[53] They have demonstrated the successful encapsulation of Au metal NPs inside the cavities of microporous ZIF-8 and ZIF-90 through the gas phase infiltration of $[\mathrm{Au(CO)Cl]}$ followed by a thermal hydrogenation of the $[\mathsf{A u}(\mathsf{C O})\mathsf{C l l}]@\mathsf{Z I F}$ composite at $100{-}130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to convert it to $\mathrm{Au}@Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{IFḌ}$ . Interestingly, the nature of the linkers of the parent ZIFs could influence the particle size distribution by stabilizing the formed metal NPs. Specifically, the Au NPs encapsulated in the cavities of ZIF-90 exhibited a much narrower size distribution $(>95\%$ , ${\le}2\mathrm{nm}$ ) with a uniform mean diameter of $1.4\mathrm{nm}$ , compared to that in the case of ZIF-8 (i.e., broad size distribution, ranging from $1{-}5\mathrm{nm}$ ). Furthermore, for the case of ZIF-90, the size of the encapsulated Au NPs could better match the cavity size of ZIF-90 $[1.2\mathrm{nm}]$ ), whereas for the case of ZIF-8, the size of the encapsulated NPs was 2–4 times larger than the dimensions of the ZIF-8 cavity $(1.2\ \mathrm{nm})$ ). Their Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy analysis of the intermediate $[\mathrm{AuCl}]@\mathrm{ZIF}\cdot90$ revealed the presence of surface anchoring of the Au-Cl species at the functional groups, which was likely to block their mobility and hindered subsequent particle growth during the hydrogenation step. This is largely different from the case of ZIF-8 where the formation of Au NPs already started parallel to the loading process, hence resulting in larger-sized Au NPs.
When used as catalysts for the aerobic oxidation of benzyl alcohol (BA) to methyl benzoate (MB), the $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ core–shell particles exhibited a good conversion efficiency of $81\%$ and a high selectivity of $98\%$ . Unexpectedly, despite the better cavitysize matching, the $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}\cdot90$ particles displayed a much lower conversion efficiency of $13\%$ and a moderate selectivity of $50\%$ . The poor catalytic performance of the $\operatorname{Au}@Z\operatorname{IF}\cdot90$ composite was attributed to the in situ oxidation of the aldehyde functions of ZIF-90 taking place in parallel to the conversion of BA to MB. The encapsulated Au NPs could catalyze the oxidation of the nearby imidazolate-2-carboxyaldehyde groups to imidazolate2-methylcarboxylate in methanol under oxygen-rich environment. This observation suggests the importance of considering the possible reaction between the intercalated metal NPs and organic linkers when developing metal NPs@ZIF catalysts.
Method II: Encapsulation of Surfactant-Stabilized Metal NPs: Pre-synthesized metal NPs that are capped by stabilizers can be encapsulated in ZIFs by introducing the metal NPs into a precursor solution containing the molecular building blocks for ZIFs.[49] When individually capped metal NPs are encapsulated inside ZIFs, the particle size of the metal NPs can be easily controlled, and aggregation of NPs can be minimized in the host framework. The controlled encapsulation of Pt and Au NPs within ZIF-8 crystals has previously been achieved by capping the encapsulated metal NPs with the surfactant poly(vinylpyrrolidone) (PVP) in a well-dispersed manner.[41] For example, Au NPs $\left(13{\mathrm{nm}}\right)$ ) have been successfully encapsulated in ZIF-8 by mixing together methanolic solutions of zinc nitrate, 2-MeIm and Au NPs solution, followed by aging for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ of reaction, the Au NPs were embedded inside the ZIF-8 particles and the initially spherical morphology of the ZIF-8 particles was changed to a rhombic dodecahedron shape (Figure 5b).
The spatial distribution of the incorporated PVP-modified Au NPs in ZIF-8 crystals can be controlled by tuning the addition sequence (either addition at the beginning $(T_{0})$ or after certain time $(T)$ during the growth process of ZIF-8, as shown in Figure 5a. When Au NPs were introduced at a certain time after the initiation of the formation of ZIF-8 (e.g., after $15~\mathrm{min}$ ) instead of at the beginning, $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ hybrid particles that consisted of the nanoparticle-free cores, nanoparticle-rich transition layers and nanoparticle-free shells were obtained, as shown in Figure 5c. This observation revealed that the encapsulation process did not rely on the heterogeneous nucleation mechanism, but instead was based on the successive adsorption of PVPmodified NPs on the continuously forming fresh surfaces of the growing ZIF-8 spheres until the metal NPs were depleted. The most striking observation is that larger-sized Au particlerich core and smaller Au nanoparticle-rich transition layers and nanoparticle-free shells could be prepared by sequential addition of $34~\mathrm{nm}$ Au particles at the beginning and $13~\mathrm{nm}$ Au particles after $40\mathrm{mi}$ (Figure 5d,e). Using a similar strategy, $3.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ Pt NPs containing different amounts of Pt and $180\ \mathrm{nm\PVP}$ - modified PS spheres could also be encapsulated. This unique formation strategy of hybrid crystals that contain cores rich in one type of NPs and transition layers rich in another type of NPs are essential for achieving a regioselective process where the size of the NPs in core and the outer layer can cause a significant difference. Undoubtedly, non-agglomerated fashion and controlled spatial distribution of the NPs within the ZIF-8 matrix can give rise to molecular sieving behavior which resembles the functional behavior of the encapsulated NPs.
Core–shell $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ nanowires have been synthesized by reacting PVP-capped Ag nanowires $(90-120\ \mathrm{nm})$ with the ZIF-8 precursors $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ and 2-MeIm) in methanol with stirring at $15~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ , before being reacted for another $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ in the absence of stirring to deposit ZIF-8 shell on the surface of the Ag nanowires.[55] However, the surface area of the $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{ZIF}\cdot8$ nanowires was considerably lower than that of the pure ZIF-8 crystals due to the contribution of the solid Ag core nano­wires to the total mass of the $\operatorname{Ag@ZIF}.8$ core–shell composites. Despite this drawback, the pore size of the $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ nano­ wires $(1.4~\mathrm{nm})$ was relatively close to that of the pure ZIF-8 crystals $(1.8\ \mathrm{nm})$ . The fabricated $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{ZIF}\cdot8$ core–shell nanowires exhibited a superior uptake ability toward 1-butanol to pure $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanowires due to the hydrophobic nature of the ZIF-8 shell.
A novel method of fabricating metal $\mathrm{NPs@ZIF}$ composites was demonstrated by the Tsung group, in which cuprous oxide $\left(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ NCs were used as sacrificial templates for the formation of Pd octahedra $@$ ZIF-8 composites.[52] The synthetic strategy involved firstly coating the Pd NC cores with a layer of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to form $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ core–shell structures. After the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ coating, the $\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ composites were mixed with 2-MeIm and zinc nitrate in methanol. Following the decrease in the solution pH due to the deprotonation of 2-MeIm during the formation of ZIF-8, the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ shell was etched off simultaneously. The residual $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ could be completely removed with a solution of $3\%$ ammonium hydroxide $(\mathrm{NH_{4}O H})$ in methanol, which yielded $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ core–shell heterostructures. When evaluated as catalysts for the hydrogenation of ethylene, cyclooctene, and cyclohexene, the catalytic test results showed some interesting trends. For the cyclooctene hydrogenation, the $\operatorname{Pd}(\varnothing)$ ZIF-8 catalyst showed poor catalytic activity but good activity in ethylene hydrogenation. Such phenomena were attributed to the molecule size-selective property of the ZIF-8 shell. Ethylene molecules $(2.5\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ are sufficient small to diffuse through the pore apertures of the ZIF-8 shells (3.4 Å) without any difficulty. On the contrary, the cyclooctene molecules (5.5 Å) are much larger than the pore apertures of the ZIF-8 shells. Hence, only the catalyst in which the Pd NCs were directly deposited on the external surface of ZIF-8 (Pd on ZIF-8), rather than encapsulated inside the ZIF-8 showed activity for cyclooctene hydrogenation. Interestingly, the synthesized $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{ZIF}.8$ catalyst showed good activity for cyclohexene hydrogenation, which has a comparable size to the aperture of ZIF-8, thus indicating the flexibility of the ZIF-8 framework.
## 2.2.3. Encapsulation of Bimetallic Metal Alloy Nanocrystals in ZIFs
In recent times, some important progress has been made in the encapsulation of metal NPs into ZIFs by incorporating bimetallic alloy NCs into the cavities of ZIFs rather than individual monometallic NPs. Bimetallic alloy NCs are known to exhibit better catalytic properties than monometallic NPs largely because of the synergistic effects between the two different types of metal NPs as a result of their interplay of electronic and lattice effects.[48,60]
The Xu group has demonstrated the encapsulation of $\mathrm{Au}@\$ Ag core–shell NCs in ZIF-8 by using $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ and $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ as substrates to deposit $\mathrm{Ag}$ and Au shells, respectively.[50] The proposed method is based on a sequential deposition-reduction method, in which $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ or $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ was firstly added into a methanolic solution of hydrogen tetrachloroaurate (III) tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}.4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ or silver nitrate $(\mathsf{A g N O}_{3})$ before being reacted with sodium borohydride $(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4})$ to reduce the metal salt to metal NPs, leading to the formation of $\mathsf{A g@A u}/$ ZIF-8 or $\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{Ag}/\operatorname{ZIF}~\xi$ composites. Surprisingly, regardless of the order of the deposition (i.e., whether using Au $@$ ZIF-8 or $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ as the initial precursor), the final composites were found to exhibit a similar composition. When employed as a catalyst for reduction of 4-nitrophenol, the $\operatorname{Au}\ @\operatorname{Ag}/\operatorname{ZIF}{-}8$ composite showed a higher catalytic activity than $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Ag}}$ alloy NCs,Au $@$ ZIF-8, and $\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8$ due to the synergistic catalytic effect of Au and $\operatorname{Ag}$ species and the modification of the electronic structure in a core–shell which is superior to that in an alloy or monometallic NP for the reduction of 4-nitrophenol. A slightly modified approach has been used to fabricate $\mathrm{\sfAgPd/}$ ZIF-8 composites, in which activated ZIF-8 was added into methanolic solutions ${\tt A g N O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PdCl}_{4}$ and reacted for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to impregnate metal salts before being reduced with $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ to generate the bimetallic alloy NPs inside the ZIF-8 NCs.[56] The resultant $\operatorname{AgPd}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{ZIF}.8$ catalyst particularly $\mathrm{Ag}_{18}\mathrm{Pd}_{82}$ showed a much higher catalytic activity than $\operatorname{Ag}({\widehat{\mathbb{Q}}})$ ZIF-8 toward dehydrogenation of formic acid with the turnover frequency (TOF) value of $580~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , and $100\%$ hydrogen selectivity at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Interestingly, when the Pd content was further increased, the catalytic activity decreased, thus, highlighting the synergistic effect of molecular-scale AgPd alloy composition in ZIF-8 for the catalytic dehydrogenation of formic acid.
A PdAu $@$ ZIF-8 core–shell hybrid could be prepared by dispersing preformed surfactant-stabilized bimetallic PdAu NPs in methanolic solution of 2-MeIm followed by the addition of zinc nitrate to promote the encapsulation of PdAu NPs by the growing ZIF-8 NCs.[57] The resulting $\mathrm{PdAu}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ hybrid exhibited a lower BET surface area of $1198~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ compared to ZIF-8 $(1544~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , $\operatorname{Au}@Z\operatorname{IF}-8$ $(1278~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{Pd}@Z\mathrm{IF}{\cdot}8$ $(1252\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , as a result of the incorporation of non-porous PVPcoated NPs. Unexpectedly, the $\mathrm{PdAu}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ hybrid showed poor catalytic activity for liquid phase aerobic alcohol oxidation due to the hydrophobic nature of the formed ZIF-8 shell, which restricted access to the reactants and isolated the NPs from the aqueous phase. This finding suggests that the encapsulation of bimetallic alloy NCs in more hydrophilic and water stable MOF matrices with larger pore openings may give rise to more active catalysts, which in turn may provide the synergetic combination of the molecular-sieve function of the MOF with the high catalytic activity of the bimetallic alloy NPs. A summary of the various metal NPs and bimetallic metal NCs which have been encapsulated inside ZIFs to form hybrid materials is provided in Table 1.
To summarize, the well-directed organization of metal NPs and bimetallic alloy NCs inside the cavities of spherical ZIF-8 NCs largely depends on several factors which include: i) shape and size of the substrate to be encapsulated, ii) continuous phase surface adsorption behavior of the substrate which determines the position of the substrate in the ZIF-8 encapsulated composites, and iii) contribution of polar and non-polar counterparts in surface adsorption during growth process of ZIFs.[36] From the studies presented in this section, it is clear that metal NPs can be well-confined with ordered dispersion into the ZIF frameworks and they can subsequently impact the growth, shape and size of the ZIF-8 NCs. Thus, future developments in the encapsulation strategies of metal NPs and bimetallic alloy NCs into ZIFs may open avenues for imparting new functionality or enhancing the functionality of ZIF materials.
## 2.3. Strategy III: Encapsulation of Biomolecules in ZIFs
## 2.3.1. Encapsulation of Enzymes and Proteins
The coupling of MOFs with enzymes in which the enzyme mole­ cules are either physically adsorbed or covalently bound to the pre-synthesized host can impart molecularly specific functions to the enzymes. In the case of using MOF with microporous windows for encapsulating an enzyme with a larger dimension than its normal window or cage size, the enzyme has to be encapsulated by constructing the MOF in the presence of the enzyme, otherwise the conformation change of protein can cause it to travel through the nanopores.[18] However, such phenomenon is not yet known for ZIF materials. To date, there are two main methods which have been used to encapsulate enzymes in MOFs, namely de novo encapsulation (including co-precipitation and biomineralization) and post-synthetic encapsulations (mainly used for channel-type and cage-type MOFs), as shown in Figure 6a.
Highly functional ZIF-enzyme composites have been synthesized by employing a de novo approach using ZIFs with pore size smaller than the size of the mixture of two or more enzymes. For instance, ZIF-90 with an average pore size of ${\approx}1\ \mathrm{nm}$ could be used to selectively encapsulate the catalase enzyme with an average size of ${\approx}10\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 6b).[61] This unique concept of size-selective encasing is shown to increase the tolerance of the embedded enzyme from other factors such as protease. The true embedding of the enzyme catalase in this case can be demonstrated by comparing the gel-electrophoresis behavior with a fluorescent-labeled catalase. In spite of the lower rate of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ degradation of catalase $@$ ZIF-90 in the presence of protease by the encasing effect of ZIF-90 and due to the mass-transport limitations or non-optimized interfaces between ZIF-90 and catalase, the de novo aqueous embedding method can lead to a more active enzyme functional composite as compared to an embedding method which uses conventional ZIF-90 synthesis in ethanol which may denature catalase or other embedded enzyme. This type of size-encasing function to protect an enzyme from inhibitors may offer a novel tool to immobilize and impart new functions to biomolecules (DNA, RNA, proteins) for biomedical applications.
Generally, protein molecules are directly embedded on the surface of MOFs by co-precipitation method. For example, the embedding of cytochrome c (Cyt c) protein on the surface of ZIF-8 has been achieved with the assistance of PVP as dispersant and stabilizer of protein in organic media (Figure 6c).[62] A morphological evolution of ZIF from rods to rhombic dodecahedron crystals (Figure 6c(1)) was observed during the formation of Cyt $c$ -embedded-ZIF-8 composite containing the same morphology as pure ZIF-8. However, the embedding of Cyt c within ZIF-8 was significantly different from a simple adsorption of Cyt $c$ on the surface of ZIF-8, as revealed from thermal experiments, which showed the existence of cavities $(5{-}20\ \mathrm{nm})$ resulting from the removal of Cyt c molecules and their aggregates, This study further revealed the existence of coordinating interactions between $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ and amide bonds of protein, which assisted the embedding of Cyt $\mathbf{\Psi}_{c}$ within ZIF-8 NCs during crystal growth. In this case, the preservation of protein configuration could be attributed to two main factors: i) incubation in Cyt $\boldsymbol{c}$ in organic media, and ii) micro-environment created by ZIF-8 and its activation effect. These two or more factors enhanced the activity of the embedded Cyt $\boldsymbol{c}$ in ZIF-8 for the decomposition of hydrogen peroxide by significantly increasing the substrate affinity by creating a micro-environment which is suitable for enzyme activity (Figure  6c(2)). The peroxidase activity of the Cyt $c@$ ZIF-8 composite in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (among other perox­ i­des) was noticeably higher than similar enzyme immobilized systems on solid carriers such as silica nanospheres, single enzyme NPs, nanogels, mesoporous silica and enzyme-inorganic hybrids.[14,63–65] We highlight this fundamental difference of the enzyme-ZIF hybrid in terms of preserving the activity of enzymes as compared to that of the other conventional hosts (silica nanospheres, nanogels, mesoporous silica, enzyme-inorganic hybrid) of enzymes. Therefore, this novel technique of embedding enzymes by creating a micro-environment and coordination interactions (instead of surface adsorption) should be greatly emphasized in the future, as the activity of the enzymes can be enhanced by multiple fold and the combination with ZIF-8 can impart selectivity to the enzymes via a size sieving effect.
## 2.3.2. Encapsulation of Drug Molecules and Fluorescent Dyes
Apart from enzymes and proteins, ZIFs have also been used to encapsulate drug molecules and fluorescent dyes. The effectiveness of ZIFs for drug-delivery applications can be improved by[66,67]:
i)	 enhancing the monodispersed nature of the ZIF NCs and ensuring that their size is sufficiently small ( ${<}100\mathrm{nm}$ for optimal size) to achieve an efficient cellular uptake;   
ii)	 preventing the formation of larger aggregates in order to avoid a premature release of the drugs, and   
iii)	enhancing their stability under aqueous physiological conditions.
However, the toxicity and release strategies of ZIFs for drug delivery have presented some concerns.
Among many ZIFs, ZIF-8 has been considered as a potential carrier for drug molecules due to its low toxicity, high biocompatibility and high chemical stability under physiological conditions. Furthermore, ZIF-8 typically decomposes under acidic conditions, which can be used to construct pH-sensitive drug delivery systems. Uniform single crystal ZIF-8 nanospheres with an average size of $70~\mathrm{nm}$ have been used as hosts for encapsulating fluorescein inside their frameworks with great cell internalization and minimum cytotoxicity.[67] By taking advantage of the ZIF-8 frameworks, magnetic features were incorporated into the drug delivery systems by encapsulating magnetic iron oxide NPs. Furthermore, the study further revealed the possibilities of in situ trapping of dye molecules inside the pores of ZIF-8 during crystal nucleation and growth. These dye-functionalized ZIFs were highly effective in releasing the fluorescein cargo from ZIF-8 spheres. Such a unique in situ trapping process of fluorescent drugs and the possible monitoring of the dispensability of fluorescein-encapsulated ZIF-8 dispersed in cell medium emphasize the possibilities of imparting novel biological properties into ZIFs by encapsulating drug molecules.
ZIF-8 has also been employed to encapsulate the anticancer drug, doxorubicin (DOX) for controlled drug delivery and release. Unlike many previous methods which relied on post-synthesis functionalization, Zou and co-workers have successfully developed a facile one-pot method which combined both the ZIF synthesis and molecule encapsulation.[68] In their proposed strategy, DOX solutions of varying concentrations $(2{-}10\ \mathrm{mg\mL^{-1}},$ ) were initially mixed with zinc nitrate aqueous solutions and the $\mathrm{\pH}$ was adjusted to 8 by using sodium hydroxide (NaOH). During this process, the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and the target organic molecules assembled to form coordination polymers. Next, a solution containing 2-MeIm was added to disassemble the zinc ions from the target organic molecules and subsequently formed ZIF-8 by the assembly of the metal ions and linkers. Simultaneously, the target molecules were encapsulated during the formation of ZIF-8, resulting in hierarchical structures. SEM characterizations of the $\operatorname{DOX@ZIF{-}}8$ materials revealed that their morphology consisted of isolated crack-free particles with diameter ranging from $70-300\ \mathrm{~nm}$ . Furthermore, the mesopores became more homogeneously distributed within the ZIF-8 crystals when the loadings of DOX were high $(214\%)$ and the size of the mesopores was increased with the loading of DOX. Despite the lack of morphological change of the ZIF-8 crystals with increasing loading of DOX (from $0\%$ to $20\%$ ), the particle size was gradually increased with the size distribution broadened from $100\pm30$ to $230\pm150\ \mathrm{nm}$ , according to the dynamic light scattering (DLS) measurements.
The resulting DOX $@$ ZIF-8 particles were employed as an efficient pH-responsive drug delivery system as the composite particles could prevent the release of the drug at physiological condition (PBS, pH 7.4) but released the drug in a controlled manner at low pH (5.0–6.5). Interestingly, the addition of ZIF-8 altered the delivery pathway of DOX in MDA-MB-468 breast cancer cell. Free DOX molecule was quickly accumulated in the nuclei, whereas the DOX $@$ ZIF-8 particles were localized mainly in cytoplasm and sub-cellular organelles. In terms of cytotoxicity, the core–shell particles exhibited higher cytotoxi­ city to the breast cancer cells than pure ZIF-8, free DOX, and a mixture of ZIF-8 and DOX, due to the synergistic effect of ZIF-8 and DOX. These results highlight the potential of ZIFs as effective carriers for drug molecules for targeted delivery and controlled release, although the increased cytotoxicity may need to be addressed in future studies.
As discussed in this section, ZIFs have been shown to be highly versatile materials for hosting various guest species, such as highly active metal NPs and bimetallic alloy NCs, enzymes, and drug molecules, for the purpose of developing functional materials out of ZIFs. In most cases, the encasing of guest species can be achieved either by embedding them inside the ZIF NCs or by immobilizing them on the surface of the ZIF particles. In some cases, ZIF particles are grown as shells on the core guest species. The later strategy is beneficial as it allows for a smaller window size of the ZIF walls as compared to the size of guest species, which is advantageous for biomedical applications.
## 3. Size and Morphological Control of ZIFs
In order to optimize the functional properties of ZIFs, it is important to modulate various factors such as particle size, pore size of crystalline walls or windows, and surface areas, depending on the specific requirements. Shape-controlled ZIFs have attracted much attention due to the possible provision of new structural domains and the potential for exposing the highly active facets and controlling the size of ZIF crystals is important for optimizing their surface area (and nanoporous materials derived from them).
## 3.1. Size Control of ZIFs
As shown in Table 2, the synthesis conditions of ZIF crystals can have a profound effect on their particle size and surface area. Generally, the size of ZIF crystals is governed by various factors such as:
i)	 The choice of metal salts and ii) the mixing order of the ZIF precursors
The reactivity of the zinc precursors has a direct influence on the resulting size of the ZIF-8 crystals, as previously demonstrated by Schejn et al.[19] They discovered that small ZIF-8 NCs $(\approx50-200\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) could be obtained with the use of reactive zinc salts, such as $\mathsf{Z n}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{\Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{ZnSO_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{Zn(ClO_{4})_{2}}$ . On the contrary, the use of non-reactive salts, including $\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{Z n}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}$ $\mathrm{(OAc=acetate)}$ or $\mathrm{ZnI}_{2}$ yielded ZIF-8 crystals with larger sizes between $350{\mathrm{-}}650{\mathrm{nm}}$ . Similarly, the use of ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OAc}})_{2}$ during the hydrothermal synthesis of ZIF-67 NCs at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ also gave the highest average particle size $(570\ \mathrm{nm})$ compared to $\mathrm{CoSO_{4}}$ $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\mathrm{480}\mathrm{nm}}$ ), $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}$ ( $220\mathrm{nm})$ and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ ( $340~\mathrm{nm})$ ).[69]
The results of the above studies are in good agreement with our previous study, in which the replacement of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ with $\mathrm{Zn(CH_{3}O O)_{2}}$ increased the average particle size of the obtained ZIF-8 NCs from $50\mathrm{nm}$ to $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ .[70] It is known that the nucleation rate of ZIF crystals is controlled by the coordination speed between the metal ions and the organic linkers. In the formation of small-sized ZIF-8 crystals, the solvation of ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ (from $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2})$ in methanol solution is relatively weak and therefore, the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions can quickly coordinate with 2-MeIm, leading to a fast nucleation. On the other hand, replacing $\mathrm{\Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}$ with $\mathsf{Z n}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OO})_{2}$ significantly slowed down the generation speed of ZIF-8 crystals, leading to large-sized ZIF-8 crystals. This is because the dissolved $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions strongly interacted with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO^{-}}$ and this interaction along with the solvation of ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions is highly effective for preventing the rapid nucleation of ZIF-8 crystals.
In the same study, we have shown that when two dried powders of zinc nitrate and 2-MeIm were mixed together during the synthesis process (rather than separately dissolved, before being mixed), ZIF-8 particles with an intermediate average size of $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were obtained. This is because once methanol was added, the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions directly reacted with 2-MeIm, hence, there was not enough time not enough time for the solvation of $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ ions to occur. Therefore, the only interaction which occurred was the between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO^{-}}$ ions compared to the above large-sized particle system; therefore, leading to mild crystallization and the production medium-sized ZIF-8 particles.
iii)	The ratio of metal salt to 2-MeIm
Another possible method for controlling the size of ZIF NCs is by adjusting the ratio of the metal salt to the organic linker. Tanaka et al. found that in the aqueous-phase synthesis of ZIF-8 NCs, the average particle size of the generated ZIF-8 nanocrystals was decreased with increasing 2-MeIm/Zn molar ratio.[71] In addition, higher monodispersity was observed at higher 2-MeIm/Zn molar ratios compared to lower ones. It is known that the ligand, 2-MeIm can exist in two equivalent tautomeric forms, as the proton can be located on either of the two nitrogen atoms. Under appropriate basic conditions above the dissociation constant, the 2-MeIm monomer deprotonates and the generated imidazolate nitrogen atom coordinates with the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions of the adjacent unit to construct the ZIF-8 crystals. At a higher 2-MeIm concentration, the number of protons released from either nitrogen atom is higher, which could lead to a larger generation of nuclei, hence leading to a smaller particle size. It is known that during the growth process, the morphology of the ZIF-8 crystals evolved with time from cubes exposing 6 $\{100\}$ facets to intermediate shapes and finally to rhombic dodecahedra exposing 12 {110} facets, which is the most stable equilibrium morphology of ZIF-8.
## iv)	The addition of surfactants or capping agents
Another effective way to control the size of ZIF-8 NCs is to introduce surfactant into the growth solution of ZIF-8 crystals. For example, Pan et al. found the surfactant, cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) to be highly effective for controlling the size and shape of ZIF-8 NCs.[4] By increasing the concentration of CTAB from $0.0025\%$ to $0.025\%$ , the mean diameter of the ZIF-8 particles was decreased from $2.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ to $110~\mathrm{nm}$ . A similar decreasing trend in particle size with increasing surfactant concentration was observed when the CTAB was replaced with CTAC (cetyltrimethylammonium chloride). The reason behind this is because when CTAB was added to the system, the CTAB molecules became adsorbed on the ZIF-8 crystal surface, which prevented nutrients to attach to the surface. Consequently, this slowed down the growth rate to generate smaller-sized ZIF-8 particles. Similarly, Fan et al.[72] have successfully synthesized ZIF-8 NCs with a sub- $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ size by using an aqueous surfactant-mediated method with a stoichiometric $Z\mathrm{n}/2$ -MeIm molar ratio. The use of the surfactant Span 80/Tween 80 mixture could stabilize the $Z\mathrm{n}/2$ -MeIm coordination structure by screening out the attack of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ in the aqueous solution, thereby, allowing the formation of pure ZIF-8 NCs at rather low 2-MeIm/Zn ratios.
## v)	 The choice of solvent
The dependence of the particle size of ZIF NCs on the choice of solvent has been reported in several studies. For instance, Xia et al. reported an increase in the average particle size of ZIF-67 NCs from $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $1.7\upmu\mathrm{m}$ by replacing the non-polar solvent methanol with a polar solvent such as water.[73] Shieh and co-workers have systematically investigated the influence of adding different alcohols during the water-based synthesis of ZIF-90 NCs.[74] Their investigation revealed that the addition of ethanol into the ZIF-90 growth solution $\mathrm{(H_{2}O/P V P}$ $(2\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ /ICA(imidazole-2-carboxaldehyde)) yielded ZIF-90 NCs with a significantly smaller average size $(600\ \mathrm{nm})$ compared to the pure $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/\mathrm{PVP}$ system $(2.5~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ . Furthermore, the particle size was further decreased with the increase in the number of carbons in the alcohol solvent (Figure 7a), due to the gradual increase in viscosity, as depicted in Figure $76$ . The smallest particle size of ZIF-90 NCs obtained was $275\ \mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}/$ tertbutanol/PVP system.
## 3.2. Morphological Control of ZIFs
To date, many strategies have been developed to control the morphology of ZIF NCs. In general, these strategies can be divided into two types: non-templated and template-mediated methods. A summary of previous reports on shape-controlled ZIFs and their synthesis conditions and surface areas is given in Table 3.
## 3.2.1. Nontemplated Method
The nontemplated method typically relies on the direct modification(s) of one or more parameters during the synthesis of ZIF NCs, including the type of solvent, the Zn/2-MeIm ratio. Shi et al. have demonstrated the first synthesis of a highly ordered hierarchical ZIF nest architecture with a GIS topology (denoted as "ZIF-GIS"), through a solvothermal method using a mixture of aqueous ammonia $\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)$ and methanol as the solvent.[75] Structurally, the nest-like architecture was assembled from numerous nanoplates with varying thicknesses from $20{-}40~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 7c). The formation of such a highly ordered hierarchical nest architecture was attributed to the synergistic co-operation between the two solvents. Similarly, the replacement of methanol with water together with the adjustment of the Zn:2-MeIm ratio to 1:8 (i.e., excess 2-MeIm) during the synthesis of ZIF-8 crystals has been reported to generate ZIF (ZIF-L) particles (Figure 7d) exhibiting a large 0D pore or cavity with dimensions of $9.4\mathrm{~\overset{~\circ~}{A}~}\times7.0\mathrm{~\overset{~\circ~}{A}~}\times5.3$ Å.[76] Furthermore, ZIF-67 truncated dodecahedra with 18 exposed facts have also been achieved by simply substituting methanol with water and by increasing the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}/2$ -MeIm ratio to 1:100 during the room-temperature synthesis of ZIF-67 rhombic dodecahedra.[77] Despite some early studies on the control of ZIF morphology through the non-templated approach, successful reports using this method are still few and far between. However, it is clear that the solvents used during the synthesis of ZIF crystals can have a profound effect on the final morphology.
## 3.2.2. Template-Mediated Method
Compared to the template-free method, the template-mediated approach is far more versatile as it offers the possibilities of achieving a more diverse morphology of ZIF particles. In general, soft templates (e.g., surfactants and BCPs) or hard templates (e.g., PS spheres) can be used to achieve this purpose. The surfactant CTAB has previously been utilized to effectively control the crystal morphology of ZIF-8 particles For example, Pan et al. have reported that the tenfold increase in CTAB concentration from $0\%$ to $0.025\%$ resulted in the gradual transformation of ZIF-8 crystals from rhombic dodecahedron shape with 12 exposed $\{110\}$ faces to $\{110\}$ truncated cubes.[4] The growth of the ZIF-8 crystals is known to evolve with time from cubes exposing 6 $\{100\}$ facets to intermediate shapes, and finally to rhombic dodecahedra exposing 12 {110} facets, with the latter being the most stable equilibrium morphology of ZIF-8. Their molecular dynamics simulation revealed that during the crystallization of ZIF-8, CTAB molecules (i.e., the long hydrophobic hydrocarbon chain) would be much more strongly adsorbed on a $\{100\}$ facet than $\{110\}$ and {111} facets. As a result, the growth rate of the $\{100\}$ facet is significantly lower compared to the {110) and {111} facets, thereby giving rise to a truncated cubic morphology.[4] Similarly, ZIF-67 nanocubes have also been obtained by adding a certain amount $\left(1-3~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ of CTAB to the ZIF-67 growth solution $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and 2-MeIm $(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MeIm}=1;55)$ ).[78]
Highly uniform hollow ZIF-67 spheres with a mean diameter of $325~\mathrm{nm}$ have been synthesized by the mixing of $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , 2-MeIm, and alkyltrimethylammonium bromide $({\mathrm{C}}_{n}{\mathrm{TAB}}$ ; $n=12$ , 14 or 16) in an aqueous medium under room temperature conditions (Figure 7e,f).[79] In this system when $\mathbf{C}_{n}\mathrm{TAB}$ and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ are mixed with aqueous 2-MeIm solution, a tetrahedral coordinated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ complex, $[\mathrm{Co}(2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{MeIm})(\mathrm{IM})(\mathrm{L}_{2})]^{-}$ is generated. Since exposing the long hydrocarbon chain of $\mathrm{C_{14}T A^{+}}$ is thermodynamically unfavorable in water, vesicles with hydrophobic chains stacked together in a bilayer structure are likely to form, as schematically shown in Figure $\mathrm{7g(i)}$ . Furthermore, the [Co-complex]− and other anions are distributed around the $\mathrm{C}_{n}\mathrm{TA}^{+}$ ammonium head groups to balance the overall charges. Consequently, [Co-complex]− species concentrated on the surface of the vesicles act as nucleation sites for the heterogeneous outward-growth of ZIF-67 crystals (Figure $\mathrm{7g(ii)}$ ). Subsequent growth of these islands leads to the formation of a polycrystalline shell (Figure 7g(iii)). Finally, hollow ZIF-67 spheres are obtained following post-synthetic washing using DMF and methanol to remove the interior soft template.
For the nucleation of core–shell heterostructures of ZIFs, two important factors must be considered: i) how to restrain the growth of ZIFs to the core surface when the core is created with another MOF or different species, and ii) how to control the shapes of such core–shell heterostructures. The template-based method is believed to be effective for fabricating ZIF core–shell heterostructures with controlled size and morphology, however, it normally requires prior surface modification with specific functional groups on the template to confine the growth of MOFs on the surface of the template. For example, carboxyl­ate terminated PS spheres have been used as templates for the formation of core–shell $\mathrm{PS}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ composites, which could subsequently be converted to hollow ZIF-8 microspheres by removing the PS cores.[80] The uniqueness of this method lies in the formation of a secondary layer of ZIF-8 to enhance the overall thickness of ZIF-8 shell.
The BCP, polystyrene- $b$ -poly(4-vinylpyridine) (PS-b-P4VP) $(188~000{\cdot}b{\cdot}64~000~\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ has been employed as a soft template for the fabrication of hollow ZIF-8 nanoworms, as firstly reported by the Peinemann group.[20] To achieve the hollow ZIF-8 nanotubes, the BCP filament-shaped micelles solution was firstly added into the ZIF-8 precursor solution and reacted in an oil bath at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to generate hybrid BCP $@$ ZIF-8 structures. Next, the BCP $@$ ZIF-8 hybrid was dissolved in DMF to dissolve the BCP portions, yielding the filamentous ZIF-8 nanoworms. The BCP-templated method therefore offers a promising approach for obtaining uniform 1D ZIF nanostructures and should be further explored in the future to increase the diversity of ZIF morphology which can be obtained.
Considering the desired structures of MOFs with larger pores along with the micropores, the utilization of elongated organic linkers is often crucial to increase their pore size which may give rise to framework instabilities.[81] The creation of hierarchical (micro- and meso-) pores into ZIFs via supramolecular templating usually involves the stable interactions between the surfactant micelles and metal ions. When CTAB is used as a structure-directing agent and a chelating agent, Histidine containing an imidazole group played the role of a co-template.[82] Additionally, due to the chelating effect of His with $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ in the initial stage of nucleation, the formation of zinc hydroxide or other by-products can be avoided. Possible challenges associated with this strategy are: i) the hydrated $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ may be entrapped in the hydrophilic outer layers of micelles, and ii) the interaction of ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and CTAB micelles has to be directed through the His to exert the cooperative effect which creates the mesopores. The interaction of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and His with CTAB through electrostatic interactions between CTAB micelles with a positively charged head group layer and electron-rich imidazolate of 2-MeIm is crucial in the formation of such hierarchical ZIFs.
The true interconnection of multi-porosity in ZIF material is a rare phenomenon apart from microporous ZIF materials. Previously, bicontinuous hierarchical ZIF-8 nanostructures have been synthesized using a BCP templating method.[83] By removing the BCP template, $\mathrm{PSty}_{14}–b–\mathrm{P4VP}_{19}$ , after the nucleation and crystal growth of ZIF-8 around the micelles (ZIF-8-VP), branched fibrous networks consisting of $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ nanocrystallites with interconnected textural voids were obtained. In this process, all the mesopores and macropores were highly accessible and interconnected, due to electrostatic shielding and the bridging effect of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ and the interconnected textural voids gave rise to a bicontinuous architecture. This strategy of forming hierarchical ZIF-8 promises new developments towards the fabrication of hierarchical functional materials which require interconnected macro- and meso-porosity for enhancing their electrochemical performance. Certain features such as the flexible shell and the surface mesopores of ZIF allow for the favorable binding of substrates of varying sizes, including stabilizing intermediates and enhanced accessibility to the active sites. The design to impart such functionality into the ZIF structures via the installation of acidic $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ species and basic imidazolate moieties creates a synergistic effect on the binding substrates to activate cooperatively.
The construction of hierarchical ZIFs with tunable shell thicknesses remains a challenging task. If the nucleation process of ZIF NCs can be manipulated at the water-organic (e.g., 1-octanol) interface stabilized by PVP, it can give rise to a continuous shell on ZIF-8 hollow nanospheres.[84] A water-in-oil nanoemulsion can be prepared by using PVP as stabilizer and 1-octanol as the oil phase. ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions in the aqueous phase coordinate with 2-MeIm in 1-octanol at the spherical interfaces of the emulsion droplets to form ZIF-8 NCs which further grow to form nanospheres at room temperature. By controlling the concentration of 2-MeIm and the reaction time, hollow ZIF-8 nanospheres with different shell thicknesses can be obtained. Such functionalized hollow ZIF particles are particularly useful in size-selective chemical processes and selective adsorption of gases.
The fabrication of mesoporous ZIFs unlike silica, zeolites, and carbons are rather rare however, efforts are being made recently to enhance the molecular diffusion while preserving the molecular sieve properties. Among the two major synthetic strategies to obtain mesoporous-MOFs are: i) linker extension (increase in length of organic linkers), and ii) surfactant templating, both of which aimed at increasing the pore size. The use of soft templating typically involves the initial selfassembly of the surfactant molecules into micelles, before being subsequently removed to generate mesopores.[85] However, these methods depend on the small molecular micelles which are usually unstable under the synthesis conditions. The lack of control over shape, position and space distribution of mesopores is a challenge in the preparation of mesoporous MOFs, which need to be immediately solved. The potential applications of ZIFs can be further developed and extended upon developing a strategy to create mesopores within the framework matrix so that the functionalized ZIFs may exhibit novel physical and chemical properties.
A general method for creating functionalized ZIFs with size-, shape-, and space-distribution-controlled mesopores has recently been developed by crafting mesopores inside the ZIFs through encapsulation of NPs followed by etching.[85] Using this method, the mesopore morphology, hierarchical structure, and space distribution could be tuned by varying the size and shape of the NPs and encapsulation conditions. Hierarchical ZIFs with core-rich in large sized $13.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ mesopores and the transition layer rich in smaller $(3.8\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) mesopores have been obtained by sequential encapsulation of Au NPs with varied diameters followed by etching. Interestingly, two different types of NPs such as Au and Pt NPs could be incorporated simultaneously into ZIF-8 and etched to generate mesoporous ZIF-8. In this process, one type of NPs acted as a sacrificial template for the formation of mesopores whereas the other functioned as catalytic active sites inside ZIF-8. The main advantage of this strategy is that the obtained mesoMOFs–NPs composites cannot only maintain the well-defined crystal structures of the ZIF-8 parent but also preserve its microporosity. This rational design of meso-ZIFs with tunable mesopores, shapes and functional species such as Pt NPs, Au NPs, etc. can further expand their potential applications in catalysis and biomedicine.
Recently, mesoscopic architectures of ZIF-8 in both 2D and 3D consisting of nanoparticle cores along with microporous shells and a cavity in between have been achieved by spatially controlling the nucleation site of ZIF-8.[52] This kind of yolk– shell structure of ZIF-8 NCs obtained via sacrificial templating typically involves the surface-assisted formation of polycrystalline ZIF-8. Such hollow multi-component ZIF-8 superstructures have been demonstrated to exhibit excellent sorption properties, however, the compositional complexities of such hollow ZIF structures are yet to be explored.
## 4. Hybridization of ZIFs with Polymeric Matrix (Mixed Matrix Membranes (MMMs))
Even though ZIF particles have shown promising properties for gas and liquid separations, they are often present as dry powders, which are unfavorable for industrial scale applications. To improve the practical application of ZIF for gas and liquid separations, they can be incorporated as additives into polymeric matrices to form MMMs. MMMs are typically formed by dispersing highly selective molecular sieve particles in polymeric matrix. As such, there are several factors which must be considered when designing MMMs for gas or liquid separations, such as: i) the compatibility between the molecular sieve and the polymeric matrix, including the adhesivity to one another, ii) the pore size of the molecular sieve particles, and iii) the dispersity of the molecular sieve particles throughout the polymeric matrix.[86] Traditionally, MMMs employ rigid hydrophilic zeolites or carbon molecular sieve particles as the molecular sieving phase.[21] However, these materials tend to be poorly compatible with glassy polymers. Poor compatibility between the polymer and the molecular sieving material may result in voids at their interface, which can subsequently reduce the selectivity as well as the overall separation performance of the MMMs.[1] ZIFs have been proposed as a potential replacement for zeolites in MMMs, due to their molecular sieving properties, which resemble zeolites Additionally, unlike zeolites which are hydrophilic (in the absence of modifications), the imidazolate linkers present in the frameworks of ZIFs make the material more hydrophobic than zeolites.[86,87] As a result, ZIFs may form a better contact with the polymeric matrix compared to zeolites. Therefore, the use of ZIF/polymer MMMs can combine the easy processability of polymeric membranes with the superior separation performance of ZIFs.[21] This promising approach represents another method of improving the functionality of ZIFs for industrial applications.
To date, ZIFs have been used as additives in various polymeric matrices such as polyimides (Matrimid,[87] Ultem,[86] 6FDA-DAM[1]), polysulfone,[88] polybenzimidazole,[89] and so on. A detailed and comprehensive review on the use of MOFs in MMMs, including the theoretical aspects has been previously published by Janiak and co-workers.[21] In this section, we will only select several case studies to provide some examples of how the incorporation of ZIFs into polymers to form MMMs is an attractive route for enhancing their functional performance for gas and liquid separations.
## 4.1. ZIF-Based MMMs for Gas Separation
ZIF-based MMMs have been widely employed for the separation of various economically important gases such as hydrogen $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}\right)$ , carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ , oxygen $(\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , nitrogen $(\Nu_{2})$ , methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ (ethylene), $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ (ethane), $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ (propylene), $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ (propane), as detailed in Table $4.^{[21]}$ This is largely due to their molecular sieving properties which allow for the effective separation of a specific gas from a mixture of two or more gases. Some examples of ZIF-based MMMs for gas separation applications are highlighted below.
i)	 ZIF-90/polyimide MMMs (Matrimid, Ultem, 6FDA-DAM) for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation
ZIF-90 has been considered as an attractive MOF for application in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ selective MMMs. This is due to its SOD cage-like structure with a pore window of $0.35~\mathrm{nm}$ , through which size exclusion of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixtures is possible.[21] In addition, the imidazole linker in ZIF-90 contains a carbonyl group, which can form a chemical non-covalent interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . However, the size of ZIF-90 crystals synthesized by the conventional solvothermal synthesis is often too large $(\approx100\upmu\mathrm{m})$ to be effectively employed in MMMs. To solve this issue, Nair and co-workers introduced a novel non-solvent induced crystallization technique for effectively suppressing the size of ZIF-90 crystals to sub-micrometers.[90] By adding methanol or water during the crystallization of ZIF-90 in DMF, smaller ZIF-90 crystals with an average size of $810~\mathrm{nm}$ or $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ could be obtained. Three distinct MMMs composed of these ZIF-90 particles and three different polyimides (Matrimid, Ultem, 6FDA-DAM) ( $15\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF-7 loading for all cases) and tested for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation. All three MMMs showed higher permeability for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ than the pure polymer membranes (e.g., 1.4 to 2.9 Barrer for Ultem) without any loss in selectivity, indicating the lack of interfacial defects in these MMMs, which would have a debilitating effect on the selectivity. However, among the three MMMs, the ZIF-90/6FDA-DAM MMM showed both increased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ permeability and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity, indicating that this particular MMM was defect-free and that the permeabilities of the ZIF-7 and Ultem were excellently matched.
## ii)	 ZIF-8/Matrimid for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}$ separation
ZIF-8 shares a similar SOD topology to zeolite. Yet, unlike zeolite, whose pore size is too small to be utilized as selective adsorbents, ZIF-8 exhibits six-ring cages with aperture size of $3.4\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is close to the molecule size of various gases, such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4;\mathrm{~\scriptsize~.~}}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}.$ , $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ , etc.[1,2] Hence, they can be employed as useful inorganic additives in MMMs. For instance, Ordoñez et al. have reported the fabrication of ZIF-8/Matrimid MMMs with various ZIF loadings up to $80~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , by coating the ZIF-8 NCs with Matrimid solution in chloroform, followed by drying of the chloroform and subsequent membrane casting.[87] The achieved maximum loading of $80\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ is significantly higher than the typical loading achieved in selected zeolite materials. However, at such a very high loading, the ZIF-8/Matrimid MMM showed a major decrease in mechanical strength, leading to a reduction in flexibility. The permeability properties of the prepared MMMs were evaluated for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ and gas mixtures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The permeability of all gases was basically increased with ZIF-8 loading up to $40\mathrm{wt\%}$ . However, at higher loadings of $50\mathrm{-}60\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , the permeability decreased for all the tested gases, implying a transition from a polymer-driven gas transport process to a ZIF-8-controlled one, where at higher loadings, the sieving effect of ZIF-8 became more pronounced. On the contrary, the ideal selectivities were enhanced at higher ZIF-8 loadings for gas pairs containing small gas molecules, such as $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}$ , and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{8}$ , implying the increased influence of ZIF-8 as a molecular sieve for smaller gas molecules.
## iii)	ZIF-8/Ultem MMMs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separation
Mixed-matrix dual-layer hollow-fiber composite (asymmetric) membranes containing ZIF-8 in Ultem polymeric matrix have previously been fabricated through a dry jet-wet quench method for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm N_{2}$ separations.[86] The outer separating layer of these composite fibers contained ${\approx}17\ \mathrm{vol}\%$ of ZIF-8 filler and good adhesion was observed between the bulk polymer and ZIF-8 particles in the hybrid membrane. As a result of the good compatibility between the two components, the hybrid fibers showed increased permeance with the enhancement of permselectivity reaching as high as $20\%$ over the pure polymer. In addition, the mixed gas transport measurements revealed promising permselectivity (as high as 32) in the hybrid membranes. The results showcased the potential of ZIFs as components in MMMs for large-scale gas separations, such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture from flue gas, although the performance of the MMMs presented in this study was slightly limited by the low permeability of Ultem. MMMs made of poly(1,4-phenylene etherether-sulfone) (PPEES) and ZIF-8 with ZIF-8 filler loadings of
10, 20 and $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , have been fabricated by casting chloroform solutions containing dispersed ZIFs of different quantities.[91] When employed for $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ separations, the MMMs with $30\%$ ZIF-8 loading showed the best selectivity for the separation of both gas mixtures, suggesting the increase in the gas permeability with increasing ZIF-8 loading without a significant alteration in the selectivity.
## iv)	ZIF-8/6FDA-DAM MMMs for $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation
Koros and co-workers have employed ZIF-8/6FDA-DAM (6FDA:2,2-bis(3,4-carboxyphenyl) hexafluoropropane dianhydride; DAM: diaminomesitylene) MMMs with three different ZIF-8 loadings (low $(16.4~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , medium $(28.7~\mathrm{wt\%})$ ), and high $(48.0\mathrm{wt\%})\$ ) for $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ separation.[86] To prepare this mixed matrix dense film, a polymer solution made by dissolving 6-FDA-DAM in dichloromethane was added into the ZIF-8 dispersion in dichloromethane, followed by drying of the excess solvent and casting. It was discovered that at higher ZIF-8 loadings, clusters of ZIF-8 particles with more than an order of magnitude larger than single ZIF-8 crystals $(\approx200\ \mathrm{nm})$ were formed. Surprisingly, despite the presence of these clusters, major improvements in $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ permeability and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ selectivity were observed for the ZIF-8/6FDA-DAM MMM compared to pure 6FDA-DAM membrane by permeation tests with pure $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}}$ and $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{8}}$ as well as an equimolar $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{8}}$ mixture.
## 4.2. ZIF-Based MMMs for Liquid Separation
Apart from gas separations, ZIFs have also been employed for the separation of various liquid mixtures such as propylene/ propane and water/ethanol mixtures. For example, ZIF-8 is particularly ideal for water/ethanol separation owing to its pore aperture diameter of $0.34\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is between the kinetic diameters of water $(0.296\mathrm{nm})$ ) and ethanol $(0.40\mathrm{-}0.43~\mathrm{nm})$ ).[92]
Polybenzimidazole (PBI)/ZIF-8 MMMs with uniform morphology and high ZIF-8 loadings of up to $58\mathrm{\wt\%}$ have been developed by directly mixing the as-synthesized ZIF-8 suspension into the PBI solution for pervaporation dehydration of ethanol, isopropyl alochol (IPA) and $n$ -butanol.[89] The PBI/ ZIF-8 MMMs pervaporation test data revealed that the incorporation of $34\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF-8 into PBI membranes lead to a fourfold increase in the total flux significantly, with almost no decrease in separation factor when compared to the pure PBI membrane. Furthermore, the PBI/ZIF-8 blends were highly effective in suppressing swelling by ethanol. due to the rigidification of the PBI chains by ZIF-8 particles, thus, promoting a better separation factor.
Chitosan (CS) is one of the most widely used polymers for the fabrication of polymeric membranes, due to its hydrophilic nature, excellent formability, easy modification and good chemical stability. However, it suffers from the various drawbacks such as easy swelling in aqueous solutions, and reduced permeate water concentration due to the easy passing of the feed organic compound through the membrane.[93] To resolve these issues, microporous ZIF-7 could be incorporated into CS membrane to form ZIF-7/CS MMM to impart the high thermal and chemical stabilities and high porosity of ZIF material into them.[94] ZIF-7/CS MMMs with five different ZIF loadings of 2.5, 4, 5, 6 and $7.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ were investigated for water/ethanol separation at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The pervaporation test results showed that the ZIF-7/CS MMM with $5\mathrm{wt\%}$ loading of ZIF-7 exhibited the best separation performance with 19 times higher separation efficiency than the pure CS membrane. The increased separation efficiency compared to pure CS membrane was attributed to the polymer chain rigidification resulting from the coordination between the $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms of the ZIF-7 and the amino groups of the CS polymers and the decreased pore radius, allowing for efficient separation of water from ethanol.
Despite the advantages exhibited by ZIF-8, there are seve­ ­ral challenges for synthesizing ZIF-8-based MMMs such as: i) achieving good dispersion of the ZIF-8 particles in the polymer matrices without significant agglomeration, and ii) avoiding the formation of non-selective interfacial voids between the polymers and ZIF-8 particles. To overcome these issues, the Wu group has recently developed a drying-free, water-based process for the fabrication of ZIF-8/poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA) MMM with outstanding performance for water/ ethanol separation.[92] The proposed water-phase synthesis is highly advantageous as water molecules can ensure the good distribution of ZIF-8 NPs in the PVA matrix, thus making ZIF-8 NPs compatible with the solvent and also because PVA can be dissolved in water. In contrast to the conventional method which involves the drying and redispersion of ZIF-8 and results in cracking and phase-separated MMMs (Path A in Figure 8a), this approach does not require a drying step, and generates a transparent, crack-free MMM with well-dispersed ZIF-8 NPs (Path B in Figure 8a). When employed for the dehydration of an ethanol/water mixture $(90{:}10~\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{w})$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the PVA/ZIF-8 MMM with $39\mathrm{wt\%}$ ZIF-8 loading showed a significantly larger permeability flux and separation factor values of $2.07\times10^{6}$ Barrer and 4725, respectively compared to the pure PVA membrane $(0.75\times10^{6}$ Barrer and 543, respectively) and the permeability flux was significantly increased with the increase in ZIF-8 loading from 0 to $39\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Figure 8b). Furthermore, the separation performance of these PVA/ZIF-8 MMMs exceeded many previous ZIF-based MMMs (Figure 8c). The significant enhancement of the separation performance was attributed to the large increase in fractional free volume (Figure 8d) and the absence of interfacial voids as a result of the good adherence of PVA to ZIF-8 (Figure 8e).
In summary, the addition of ZIF NCs as additives to polymeric matrices to form MMMs have lead to significant enhancements in both gas or liquid permeability and separation performance compared to zeolite-based MMMs, mainly due to: i) improved adhesion between the ZIF particles and the polymeric matrix, and ii) rigidification of the polymer chains, leading to significant reduction of interfacial voids which would have reduced the selectivity of the MMMs. However, despite these advantages, occasionally some clusters of ZIF particles can be seen within the polymeric matrix which may lead to a slight reduction in the separation performance. To solve this issue, many recent studies have implemented a secondary carbon-based additive or filler into the polymeric matrix such as graphene oxide and CNTs, which can greatly enhance the dispersion of ZIF-8 particles throughout the polymeric matrix, leading to better gas or liquid mixture separation.[95,96] It is expected that this emerging technique will be further developed in the future to optimize the potential of ZIF-based MMMs. Moreover, the inclusion of MOFs such as ZIFs into nanostructured composite materials such as in the case of MMMs definitely impacts their mechanical properties in ways that we are only beginning to understand. Very recently, the Coudert group has successfully utilized the finite element method (FEM) to predict the effective bulk moduli of the MOF, ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53$ (Al) adsorbents dispersed in polysulfone matrix at different volume fractions.[97]
Furthermore, their simulations revealed that the concentration and position of the MOF adsorbents within MMMs could have significant effects on the their mechanical properties. It is expected that such numerical simulations may open up avenues for increasing our understanding of the impact of the inclusion of MOF adsorbents on the mechanical properties of MMMs. This numerical FEM simulation approach is readily applicable to predict the flexibility of experimental systems and it can also be extended to anisotropic composite systems, whose behavior is too complex for analytical resolution alone.
## 5. Conversion of ZIFs into Nanoporous Hybrid Materials
In the past few years, MOFs including ZIFs have attracted significant attention as versatile templates for the derivations of nanoporous materials such as carbons, oxides, and more recently, sulfides and phosphides and their hybrids. This is largely due to the presence of metal center and organic linkers in ZIFs, as well as their attractive characteristics which include exceptionally high surface areas and tunable composition and porosity.[98] Figure 9 showcases the versatility of ZIFs as starting materials for the derivations of nanoporous materials. By simply modifying the heat treatment conditions, ZIF-67 can be converted into nanoporous materials of various compositions, including porous carbons, cobalt oxide $(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4})$ , cobalt sulfide (CoS), cobalt phosphide (CoP) and their hybrids including $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@{\mathrm{C}}$ , and $\mathrm{CoP@C}$ . Table 5 summarizes the synthesis conditions, textural characteristics and applications of many previously reported ZIF-derived carbon-based hybrid materials, as well as the more recent ZIF-derived metal sulfides and phosphides. In this section, we will focus only on the derivation of nanoporous hybrid materials from ZIFs rather than individual materials. Excellent reviews covering the synthesis and applications of ZIF-derived porous carbons, oxides and others have been published elsewhere.[22,99–101]
## 5.1. ZIF-Derived Metal/Carbon Hybrids
In recent years, there has been a growing trend of using bimetallic ZIFs for the synthesis of nanoporous metal/carbon hybrids for electrocatalytic reactions.[98] Metal-supported N-doped carbon materials are becoming one of the most promising candidates to replace $\mathrm{Pt}$ -based catalysts, in acidic or alkaline medium. In general, the electrocatalytic activity of the N-doped carbon catalyst is dependent on the graphitic N, which determines the limiting current density, and the pyridinic N, which is likely to improve the onset potential due to the conversion of the reaction route from a $2e^{-}$ -dominated process to a $4e^{-}$ -dominated process.[102] Moreover, the channel transport properties of the carbon framework also play a crucial role in determining the electrocatalytic activity by governing the exposure degree of active sites and mass transport.[103]
Novel electrocatalysts based on highly porous Co, N-doped carbons (CNCo-n where $n=$ molar ratio of $\mathrm{Zn/Co)}$ have been fabricated from the carbonization of a series of bimetallic ZIFs (BMZIF) based on ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 with varied $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co}$ ratios as precursors.[104] This synthesis approach is highly beneficial as the BMZIF-templated CNCo composites inherited both merits of carbons from ZIF-8 and ZIF-67, possessed large surface area, high graphitization degree, as well as highly dispersed N and $\mathrm{CoN}_{x}$ active species, all of which are very useful characteristics for the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR). The CNCo- $\cdot n$ products with $n$ equal to 0, 0.2, 5,10, 20, 40, and 100 exhibited surface areas of 270, 362, 550, 1090, 1225, 1480, and $2184~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively, indicating a clearly increasing trend in the specific surface area of the CNCo hybrid with increasing $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co}$ molar ratio. When employed as an ORR catalyst, the CNCo-20 hybrid showed the highest electrocatalytic performance with an onset potential of $\approx-0.08$ V and a half wave potential of $\mathrm{\approx}-0.15\mathrm{\DeltaV},$ the values of which are comparable to those of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ electrode. The superb catalytic activity of CNCo-20 is thought to contributed by the synergistic effect of high surface area, ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ species, uniform dispersion of $\mathrm{~N~}$ and Co dopants, as well as hierarchical pores in its structure.
Even though the direct carbonization of ZIFs may provide a convenient method for obtaining metal-supported N-doped carbon materials, the fabrication of single-metal-atom-reactive sites on N-doped porous carbons with a high proportion of the metal species remains a challenging task. This is largely caused by the various reactions which take place during high temperature carbonization. However, a recent breakthrough was reported by Li and co-workers, in which stable Co single atoms (SAs) (rather than Co NPs) could be embedded on N-doped porous carbon polyhedrons with a high Co loading $(>4\mathrm{\wt\%})$ by employing bimetallic ZIF $(Z\mathrm{n}_{1}-$ $\mathrm{Co}_{1^{-}}\mathrm{BMOF}$ , $Z{\mathrm{n}}/{\mathrm{Co}}=1.1{\mathrm{.}}$ ).[103] To promote the formation of single Co atoms, these bimetallic ZIFs were calcined in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere at $800{-}900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and at such high temperatures, the organic linkers of the $\mathrm{Zn_{1}-C o_{1}}$ BMOFs decomposed to produce N-doped porous carbon and the metal nodes were reduced by the carbon. However, the increase in the calcination temperature to $1000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ could lead to the breakage of the $\mathrm{Co-N}$ coordination bonds, thus, generating Co NPs. Similarly, if the $Z\mathrm{n}/\mathrm{Co}$ ratio was decreased from 1:1 to 0.75:1, the formation of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-Co}$ bonds and therefore Co NPs could also not be avoided because of the poor confinement of Co by the $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms.
In this particular bimetallic system, the addition of an appropriate amount of $\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}$ was crucial for ensuring the replacement of a certain proportion of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ sites as the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions could act as a “barrier” to increase the adjacent distances between the Co atoms, which prevented the formation of ${\mathrm{Co}}{-}{\mathrm{Co}}$ bonds at elevated temperatures. Moreover, following evaporation of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ atoms at ${\approx}907\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the leaving $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ sites would generate free N-sites during carbonization and the remaining Co atoms could be anchored on the N-doped porous carbon. Surprisingly, the obtained ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ single sites showed superior ORR performance with a half-wave potential of $0.881\mathrm{~V},$ which is more positive than commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (0.811 V) due to the highly active metal-N–C sites. Durability tests revealed the exceptional electrochemical stability of the Co SAs with no obvious decay in half-wave potential after 5000 cycles, suggesting their high structural stability.
In a related report, the bimetallic ZIFs, $\mathrm{Zn}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mathrm{Co}_{1-\boldsymbol{x}}(\mathrm{MeIM})_{2}$ $\:(x=Z\mathrm{n}$ molar ratio in the initial bimetallic ZIF) have been used as templates for fabricating Co–N–C hybrids (Figure 10a) composed of fine Co NPs $(\approx9.5~\mathrm{nm}$ in average diameter) sparsely and uniformly embedded in N-doped porous carbon matrices (Figure 10b–e) by directly annealing them under an Ar atmosphere at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .[105] At lower ratios of $Z\mathrm{n}$ in the initial bimetallic ZIFs, severe aggregation of the Co NPs were observed, due to the insufficient spatial isolation of Co by the Zn species during carbonization. Moreover, when the starting Zn content in the bimetallic ZIF precursors was gradually increased (0, 50, 67, 80, 89, and $100\%$ , the resulting ${\mathrm{Co-N-C}}{\cdot}x$ hybrids exhibited increasing surface areas of 206, 227, 328, 484, 574, and $687~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. When the Co–N–C-0.8 hybrid particles (Zn molar content $=0.8$ ) were tested for ORR in $0.1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ , they showed remarkable ORR activity with a half wave potential value of 0.871 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) $30~\mathrm{mV}$ more positive than that of commercial $20\ \mathrm{wt}\%\ \mathrm{Pt/C)}$ and a kinetic current density $\left(J_{\mathrm{k}}\right)$ of $39.3\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $0.80\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE (3.1 times that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C)}$ (Figure 10f). These values were considerably higher than those of both carbonized ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 particles, suggesting the advantages of bimetallic ZIFs as precursors for the synthesis of metal/carbon hybrids compared to individual ZIFs. Moreover, the C–N–Co hybrid electrocatalyst also displayed exceptional stability as confirmed by the very small negative shift of only $5~\mathrm{mV}$ in the half wave potential value after 5000 continuous cycles (Figure 10g). Based on these exciting results, it is highly desirable to continue to employ bimetallic ZIFs for the derivations of porous carbon hybrids for optimizing their functional performance for energy storage and conversion applications.
## 5.2. ZIF-Derived Metal Oxide/Carbon Hybrids
Transition metal oxides (TMOs) are an important class of materials with potential applications in energy storage conversion and devices,[106] sensors,[107,108] photocatalysis,[109] and many others, due to their high electrical conductivity, high electrochemical response, low cost, ease of preparation, good safety, etc.[110] In electrochemical applications such as supercapacitors and LIBs, TMOs tend to exhibit higher specific capacitance and specific capacity, respectively, than carbon-based materials, but they have poorer stability due to large volume changes/expansion, phase change or structural collapse during cycling.[111] On the other hand, carbon-based materials typically exhibit lower specific capacitance and specific capacity, respectively, compared to metal oxides.[100] Yet, they tend to be more stable during prolonged cycling than metal oxides. Therefore, it is highly beneficial to create composites using these two materials to combine their respective advantages, whilst also promoting synergistic effects between them. More specifically, the implementation of a carbonaceous matrix to a metal oxide-based electrode cannot only improve its electrical conductivity, but also accommodate the large volume changes during charge/discharge process.[112,113]
ZIFs have been proposed as attractive precursors for the synthesis of metal oxide/carbon hybrids due to their high specific surface area and pore volume, which are beneficial for increasing the amount of active sites.[22] However, when using ZIFs as templates, it is important to optimize their heat treatment conditions, particularly with respect to the temperature and heating atmosphere(s) to achieve porous metal oxide/ carbon hybrids with the desired phase, good dispersion of metal oxide NPs throughout the carbon matrix and high structural integrity. ZIF-67 has been shown to be an effective precursor for synthesizing porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}$ -doped carbon hybrids as the Co ions and organic linkers could be converted to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NP cores and N-doped carbon shells, respectively, through a controlled two-step annealing process.[114] In the initial calcination in Ar at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the cobalt ion center in ZIF-67 was converted into ultrasmall metallic Co NPs and the organic linker was carbonized to N-doped carbon that wrapped the Co NPs (Co $@\mathrm{N}$ -doped C). By annealing the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{N}$ -doped C core–shell particles at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ , the central Co NPs were gradually oxidized to $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , while the N-doped C shell survived at this relatively low temperature. The two main components of the N-doping in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NC}$ were pyridinic and pyrollic N species. When employed as an anode for sodium-ion batteries (SIBs), the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NC}$ particles could deliver much higher initial charge and discharge capacities of 813 and $516~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, compared to the pure $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode (495 and $326~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}}$ , respectively). The higher capacity of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{NC}$ was promoted by: i) the defect rich structure and porous nature, which could trap more $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}}$ during cycling, and ii) the N doping which could provide more active sites for $\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ storage and enhanced the capacitive behavior. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{N}$ -doped C electrode also exhibited an improved cycling stability as it could deliver a much higher specific capacity of $373\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g}^{-1}$ , after 60 cycles at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , compared to only $200\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the pure $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode. The enhanced cycling stability was due to the buffering effect of the carbon matrix which helped to maintain the overall structural integrity during cycling.
The simultaneous nucleation, growth and anchoring of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ on oxidized carbon atoms $\mathrm{(Co_{3}O_{4}/C}$ composites) have previously been achieved through a controlled annealing of cobalt-based ZIF-9.[115] To induce excellent electrocatalytic features, the thermal nucleation of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ on the carbon surface is essential based on the morphology and pore network arrangement of ZIF-9. ZIF-9 possesses an SOD topology with hexagonal symmetry and is built from corner-sharing tetrahedral $\mathrm{CoN}_{4}$ units, in which the coordination bonds between the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ cations and benzimidazolate anions is among the most stable N-donor ligands. In this case, the nucleation of Co NPs would lead to the generation of large interparticular pores.
Apart from binary metal oxide $@\mathrm{C}$ hybrids, ZIFs have been employed to derive the more complex ternary metal $@$ metal oxide $@\mathrm{C}$ hybrids. For instance, novel $\operatorname{Co-CoO}(\varnothing\N$ -doped carbon hybrids have been obtained from the direct annealing of ZIF-67 crystals at $500{-}800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Ar atmosphere.[116] In this composite, Co NPs $\cdot5{-}20~\mathrm{nm})$ with surface oxidized CoO species were well-dispersed within the porous N-doped carbon matrix. The increase in the annealing temperature from 500 to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ promoted a higher reduction rate of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ to metallic Co $(\mathsf{C o}^{0})$ , leading to an increase in the Co content from $21.6\%$ to $39.7\%$ . The presence of CoO species was attributed to the partial oxidation of metallic Co NPs on the external surface of the hybrids. The resulting Co–CoO@N-doped carbon hybrids exhibited excellent catalytic performance for direct homo- and cross-coupling esterifications of primary alcohols under mild conditions, due to the synergistic effect between Co and CoO NPs and the stabilization of the NPs by the porous carbon matrix which improved accessibility and adsorption of substrates to the active sites and the diffusion of products.
Despite the possibility of deriving binary or ternary composites from direct annealing of ZIF crystals, often the derived nanocomposites exhibit disordered structures, which in turn, can affect the transportation of electrons or ions or the diffusion of reactants and products. To solve this problem, it is highly desirable to incorporate the ZIF-derived metal oxide $@\mathrm{C}$ or meta $@$ metal oxide $@\mathrm{C}$ composites into porous carbon supports to generate an open, hierarchical porous structure for easy transportation of electrons or ions. For instance, ZIF-9 has been used as a precursor to incorporate $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ hybrid NPs into a highly ordered porous carbon matrix (denoted as $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C-CM})$ .[117] To construct this complex ternary composite, multiple steps were involved: i) the carbon matrix with hexagonally ordered mesopores (CM) was firstly functionalized with carboxyl groups, to introduce ZIF-9 into the confined space of the highly ordered CM via coordination with ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions; ii) the ZIF-9–CM composite was heat treated at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Ar to convert it to $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CM}$ composite, and iii) the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CM}$ composite was finally heat treated in air at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to achieve the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ hybrid. During the initial heating stage, the Co complex could be easily decomposed and the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions segregated from the ZIF-9 framework and agglomerated to form ultrafine Co NPs on the carbon rods in the highly ordered porous CM. As time progressed, these NPs aggregated to form larger $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{C}$ NPs to decrease the surface energy. The Co NPs supported onto the CM were highly active and their surface layers could be oxidized into $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ during the heat treatment in air, thereby giving rise to the formation of $\operatorname{Co}\textcircled{a}$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ hybrid on the porous CM. When used for ORR, the $\mathrm{Co@Co_{3}O_{4}@C-C M}$ catalyst showed excellent ORR activity (1 M KOH as electrolyte), with a high ORR onset potential of $0.93{\mathrm{~V}}$ and a half-wave potential of $0.81\mathrm{V},$ which is comparable to commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst. In comparison, the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{C}\circ_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C}$ hybrid prepared directly from pure ZIF-67 crystals exhibited an onset potential of $0.85\mathrm{~V~}$ and a half-wave potential of $0.70{\mathrm{V}}.$ In addition, the $\mathrm{Co@Co_{3}O_{4}@C-C M}$ displayed high electrochemical stability, showing only a slight current loss with high retention of more than $90\%$ after $25{,}000\ \mathrm{s}$ . On the contrary, the $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst suffered from a rapid current loss with only $68\%$ retention. The enhanced ORR performance was attributed to various factors including: i) the presence of carbon shells which could facilitate the transport of electrons from the carbon support to the $\mathrm{Co}@\mathbb{C}\circ_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs, whilst also preventing the agglomeration of the Co NPs, and ii) the open, hierarchical structure of the hybrid which could ensure full contacts between the gas and electrolyte and lower the diffusion resistance.
Another example is the deposition of ZIF-67 particles into polymer–cobalt acetate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2})$ composite nanofibers to form polymer– ${\bf-}{\bf C o}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}@{\bf Z}\mathrm{IF}{\bf-}67$ core-nanofibers, which could subsequently be converted into hierarchical hollow $\mathrm{CNT}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ microtubes by a two-step annealing in $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ atmosphere at $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then, in air at $360^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .[118] The resulting $\mathrm{CNT}/\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ microtubes exhibited good rate capability, with a high specific charge capacity of $1281~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , as well as exceptional cycling stability, with high specific capacities of 782 and $577\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles at 1 and 4 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ,respectively. The hierarchical porous/hollow nature of the CNT/ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ microtubes not only enabled fast diffusion of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions but also provided sufficient contact between active material and electrolyte for the rapid charge-transfer reaction. Additionally, the tubular structures and void space within the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs could effectively accommodate large volume expansion upon cycling, thereby, maintaining the overall structural integrity. In addition, the CNTs integrated in the hierarchical microtubes could enhance the electronic conductivity of the composites, thus, improving the rate capability and electrochemical reactivity. Due to their synergistic effects, it is expected that the incorporation of ZIF-derived metal oxide $@$ carbon or metal $@$ metal oxide $@\mathrm{C}$ composites into porous carbon supports (e.g., ordered carbon matrix, graphene oxide, and CNTs) will become an increasingly attractive method for improving their functional performance in energy storage and conversion devices.
## 5.3. ZIF-Derived Metal Sulfides and their Hybrids
Apart from carbons and metal oxides, ZIFs have also been used as novel precursors to obtain porous metal sulfide nanostructures and their hybrids with the assistance of sulfur-containing sources (such as thioacetamide and sulfur powder).[98] For instance, hollow truncated zinc sulfide $(\mathrm{ZnS})$ rhombic dodecahedra (ca. $400~\mathrm{nm}$ ) have been synthesized by refluxing the ZIF-8 particles with thioacetamide at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , using ethanol containing trace water as the solvent.[119] At this temperature, $\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ ions are released from the hydrolysis of thioacetamide and reacts with ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions dissociated from $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{MeIm})_{2}$ , causing the formation of $Z\mathrm{n}S$ particles around the templates. With increasing reaction time, a gap is gradually formed between the ZIF-8 core and the ZnS shell and finally, the ZIF-8 cores are finally consumed, giving rise to hollow structures. In this system, achieving a fine control over the rate of $\mathsf{S}^{2-}$ hydrolysis and ZIF-8 dissolution was crucial for obtaining the hollow dodecahedra. As such, the proper use of solvent (i.e., ethanol with trace water) was necessary to slow down the hydrolysis of $\mathrm{S}^{2-}$ anions, whilst ensuring consistent supply of ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ cations to prevent the collapse of the hollow structures.
Transition metal sulfides have been considered as potential anode materials for LIBs due to their higher specific capacity than commercial graphite and their oxide counterparts as well as their intrinsically enhanced safety.[120] The high capacity of lithium storage is achieved through the reversible conversion reaction between ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ and sulfides that form metal NCs dispersed in the Li(S) matrix: $\mathrm{Mn^{+}(X)}+n\mathrm{e^{-}}+n\mathrm{Li^{+}}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{M^{0}}+n\mathrm{Li(X)}$ $(\mathrm{\boldmath~X~}=\mathrm{\boldmath~S~}$ , O).[121] However, they suffer from large volume changes during ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ insertion/deinsertion, which lead to loss of electrical conductivity and poor cycling stability. To improve the functional performance of ZIF-derived metal sulfide nanomaterials in LIBs and supercapacitors, a hybrid structure composed of carbon and metal sulfide synthesized from a single ZIF precursor is highly desirable. This is because the addition of a carbonaceous matrix can enhance the mechanical flexibility of the electrochemically active materials, whilst also buffering their volume changes, thus improving the cycling stability.[98]
Hollow hybrids composed of CoS embedded within porous carbon polyhedra/carbon nanotubes (CoS@PCP/CNTs) (Figure  11a–g) have been shown to exhibit enhanced electrochemical performance for LIBs.[122] This complex composite was successfully prepared from the carbonization of ZIF-67 NCs in Ar at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , in the presence of sulfur powder as the sulfur source. The synthesized $\cos{\ @\mathrm{PCP/CNTs}}$ polyhedra $(\cos@\operatorname{PCP}/\mathrm{CNTs}.600)$ displayed an exceptional high-rate capability (1038, 979, 858, and $752\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at current densities of 1, 2, 5, and $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , respectively) (Figure 11h) and a very high reversible specific capacity of ${\approx}1668~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles (Figure 11i). In this composite, the ultrafine CoS NPs $(\approx5\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) were embedded in the hollow porous carbon matrix, enabling easy access of Li ions, while providing excellent and stable electrical contact between the active material and the electrically conducting carbon additive. Moreover, the porous hollow structure of the composite could mediate the volume expansion occurring during the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ insertion/extraction. In addition, the in situ formed CNTs (catalyzed by the small CoS particles) could act as a secondary physical buffering matrix to maintain the structural integrity of the electrode, while also enhancing the overall electrical conductivity of the composite.
In a related report, hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ NPs embedded in graphitic carbon nanocages $({\mathrm{HCSP}}{\mathsf{C G C C}})$ have been synthesized by using ZIF-67 as a template.[123] To achieve such a complex composite, multiple steps were involved: i) the ZIF-67 particles were initially heat-treated in $\mathrm{Ar/H}_{2}$ flow to generate $\mathrm{CoNPs}@\mathrm{C}$ composite $_{\mathrm{(CP\subsetGCC)}}$ , and ii) the ${\mathrm{CP}}{\mathsf{C G C C}}$ was solvothermally reacted with thioacetamide at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{HCSP}}\subset{\mathrm{GCC}}$ composite exhibited an excellent electrochemical performance as a cathode for LIBs, including: very high energy density (707 W h $\mathrm{kg^{-1}}$ ), exceptional rate capability (reversible capabilities of 536, 489, 438, 393, 345, and $278\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, and $10\ \mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{i}}$ respectively), and stable cycling performance (a low capacity loss of $26\%$ even after 150 cycles at $1\mathrm{{C}})$ . The exceptional electrochemical performance of this composite was attributed to several factors including: i) the hollow and continuous graphitic carbon nanocages, which could significantly enhance the effective electrical conductivity and offer smooth conductive paths between $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ hollow NPs, and hence, reduce the particle-to-particle interfacial resistance; ii) the small mechanically isolated but electrochemically well-connected $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ NPs, which could prevent their aggregation during charge/discharge process, thus, enhancing their reversibility and cycling stability; and iii) the void space existing between the hollow interior of both carbon nanocages and $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ NPs along with the porosity in the polyhedral shell, which could effectively mediate volume expansion induced by the insertion/ extraction of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and enabled easy penetration of the electrolyte into the nanocages, therefore, providing a high-speed transportation channel for $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions.
## 5.4. ZIF-Derived Metal Phosphides and their Hybrids
Transition metal phosphides (TMPs) are an important class of materials which are commonly used as electrocatalysts for hydrogen (HER) and oxygen evolution reactions (OER), anode materials for LIBs and SIBs as well as catalysts for hydrodenitrogenation and hydro-desulfidation.[124] Typically, TMPs can be prepared by: i) solution-phase methods (hydrothermal solvothermal, sonochemical) by using hyphophospites tri$n$ -octylphosphine (TOP), tri-n-octylphosphine oxide (TOPO), organic phosphines (e.g., tri-phenylphosphine (TPP), white phosphorus or red phosphorus) as the phosphorus (P) source; ii) gas-phase methods utilizing hypophosphites $\mathrm{(NH}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{NaH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{2})$ ; iii) direct reduction of metal orthophosphates in $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , iv) cathodic electrodeposition, or v) pyrolysis of P-containing ionic polymers.[98,125]
Electrocatalytic water splitting, a combination of the HER and OER to generate hydrogen and oxygen, respectively, is regarded as one of the most promising methods for energy conversion. To reach this goal, the development of efficient catalysts to accelerate the kinetics of both half-cell reactions is crucial. While, precious metals (Pt) and noble metal oxides $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2})$ exhibit the best activity for the HER and OER, respectively, their large-scale industrial application is limited due to their high cost and scarcity.[106] Therefore, the development of cost-effective catalysts with high activity and durability for HER and OER is highly desirable. In general, HER and OER catalysts should possess these desirable characteristics, including: i) low cost, ii) high abundance, iii) high catalytic activity; iv) high surface area to minimize the loading amount of the catalyst, v) high electrical conductivity at high overpotentials (where oxidation potentials can change compared to the equilibrium state) to minimize the drop in Ohmic potential, vi) high corrosion stability, and/or vii) optimized pore size for minimizing the gas bubble growth and detachment of small water bubbles during water electrolysis.[124]
TMPs have been considered as one of the more attractive electrocatalysts for HER or OER, because of their high abundance, low cost, and their high acid–base stability in the pH range of 0–14.[126] Very recently, ZIFs were found to be feasible precursors for the synthesis of nanoporous TMP materials with large surface area and pore volume for developing efficient HER and OER electrocatalysts. For example, hollow CoP poly­ hedra have been prepared by the initial annealing of ZIF-67 polyhedra in air to achieve $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ phase, followed by phosphidation in an inert atmosphere at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with ${\mathrm{NaH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{2}$ to achieve the conversion to CoP phase.[126] In this case, the formation of the CoP phase is promoted by the reaction between $\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ released from the decomposition of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ at $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and metal oxides according to the equation:
$$
2\mathrm{NaH_{2}P O_{2}}=\mathrm{PH_{3}}+\mathrm{Na_{2}H P O_{4}}
$$
The benefit of using ZIFs as precursors for the preparation of porous TMP nanomaterials is highlighted by the significantly larger surface area of the ZIF-67 derived hollow CoP polyhedra compared to CoP NPs prepared by conventional phosphorization of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ particles. Furthermore, when evaluated as electrocatalysts for HER and OER, the hollow CoP polyhedra showed superior electrocatalytic performance compared to CoP particles and comparable performance to those of commercial noble-metal catalysts for both HER and OER in acidic and alkaline media, with onset overpotentials of 35 and $300~\mathrm{{mV},}$ Tafel slopes of 59 and $57~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , and a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at low overpotentials of 159 and $400~\mathrm{{mV}}$ for HER and OER, respectively. The higher HER and OER activities of the hollow CoP polyhedra are attributed to several factors including: i) the larger surface area which may provide more catalytically active sites and facilitate the transfer of electrons and masses; and ii) the reduction of surface oxidized layer on the CoP hollow polyhedra, which improved diffusion of reactants and products.
While MOF-derived TMP nanomaterials have shown potential for HER and OER, their electrical conductivity is still not satisfactory. A common strategy to facilitate this problem is to combine them with highly conductive carbonaceous materials such as reduced graphene oxide (rGO). For instance, a high performance bifunctional electrocatalyst for water splitting based on rGO/CoP has been fabricated by depositing ZIF-67 on GO sheets followed by pyrolysis in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ at $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and subsequent oxidation in air at $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to obtain the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ rGO composite, before being phosphidized at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ to achieve the $\mathrm{CoP/rGO}$ composite.[126] The resultant $\mathrm{CoP/rGO}$ composite showed an excellent HER activity in an acidic solution (0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\mathrm{,}$ ), with an HER onset potential of $13~\mathrm{mV}$ and a kinetic current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at a low overpotential of $105~\mathrm{mV}$ with a corresponding Tafel slope of $50~\mathrm{mV}$ dec−1. Furthermore, when evaluated in an alkaline solution $(1~\mathrm{~M~}~\mathrm{KOH})$ , this composite exhibited a low overpotential of $150\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , superior to those of CoP, rGO and bare glass carbon electrode (GCE) and a Tafel slope of $38~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , comparable to that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ . Interestingly, when employed as an OER electrocatalyst under alkaline condition $(1\mathrm{~M~}\mathrm{KOH})$ , the ZIF-67 derived $\mathrm{CoP/rGO}$ composite could reach a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at an overpotential of $340~\mathrm{mV}$ with a Tafel slope of $66~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , which is superior to CoP, rGO and even $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ , the state-of-the-art OER catalyst. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{CoP/}$ rGO composite also displayed an excellent durability with little decrease in the catalytic activity after 3000 cycles. The excellent electrocatalytic performance was mainly attributed to the synergistic effect between the CoP polyhedrons and the rGO support. The presented studies in this section clearly highlight the promising potential of ZIF-derived TMP nanostructures and their hybrids as bi-functional electrocatalysts for water splitting. In the future, however, it may be necessary to improve the safety aspect of TMP-based electrodes for use in electrolyzers due to their toxic nature.
In summary, this section has discussed the variety of functional nanoporous materials and hybrids which have been obtained from the framework pyrolysis of ZIFs. While there have been many successful reports on the derivation of porous hybrid materials from ZIFs (Table 5), almost all of these hybrids are carbon-based hybrids, which are obviously useful for many energy and environmental applications. In the future, it may be worth to consider the potential of deriving novel non-carbon based hybrids from ZIFs, either with or without the use of a secondary precursor.
## 6. Perspective and Summary
The diverse nanoscale structures of ZIFs offer excellent functionality and textural features for potential applications in adsorption, catalysts, gas separation, electrochemical energy storage, solar energy conversion, sensing, and electronic devices. To date, several different strategies have been developed to enhance the functionality of ZIFs such as linker modification and functional hybridization by encapsulating metal, metal oxide NPs, and even biological molecules (enyzmes/ proteins, fluorescent dyes, drug molecules). Such encapsulations are highly useful for imparting novel chemical/biofunctionality into ZIFs. The utilization of ZIFs as supports for metal NPs have several advantages over traditional porous materials including high structural integrity, moderate thermal stability, hierarchical pore networks, and the existence of organic linkers which is beneficial for stabilizing the metal NPs. However, during the encapsulation of metal NPs, it is crucial to avoid blockage of the pores of ZIFs by the metal NPs to maintain the high surface area of the ZIF support. In recent times, bimetallic alloy NCs encapsulated in ZIFs were reported to exhibit superior catalytic activity than monometallic metal NPs due to the synergistic effects between the two different types of metal NPs as a result of their interplay of electronic and lattice effects. Therefore, future research efforts into the development of novel strategies for encapsulating bimetallic alloy NPs inside ZIFs may open up new avenues for optimizing their catalytic activity without compromising their structural integrity.
The encapsulation of biomolecules such as enzymes or proteins inside ZIFs can provide many advantages including: i) imparting molecularly specific functions to ZIFs, which may be beneficial for industrial biocatalysis, biosensing and biomedical devices, ii) the provision of protective coatings because of the excellent chemical and thermal stability and low cytotoxicity of ZIFs, and iii) ZIFs can be synthesized under mild biocompatible conditions which allow for the preservation of enzymatic activity. Post-synthetic encapsulation has been widely used to encapsulate enzymes into ZIFs, in which the enzyme molecules are either physically adsorbed or covalently bound to the ZIF supports. Therefore, the pore aperture of the ZIF support needs to be sufficiently large to enable the diffusion of enzyme molecules through the host material. The recently developed de novo approach has enabled the encapsulation of enzymes into ZIFs (e.g., ZIF-90) with small pores instead of large pores, thus, broadening the potential of ZIF-encapsulated enzymes for bioapplications. In recent years, ZIFs have been utilized as inorganic additives into polymeric matrices for the creation of mixed matrix membranes (MMMs). ZIF/polymer MMMs combine the easy processability of polymeric membranes with the superior gas or liquid separation performance of ZIFs. This promising approach represents an important way of improving the functionality of ZIFs for industrial applications. An interesting development in the fabrication of ZIF-based MMMs is the combination of ZIFs with a secondary carbon-based additive or filler such as CNTs or graphene oxide in MMMs to improve the dispersion of the ZIF particles within the polymeric matrix and therefore, to improve the overall separation performance of ZIF-based MMMs. Furthermore, the incorporation of MOFs such as ZIFs into MMMs can affect their mechanical properties in ways that we are only beginning to understand. Recently, numerical finite element modeling has been successfully used to predict the mechanical properties of MOF adsorbents in MMMs at different volume fractions and positions. It is expected that the use of such numerical simulations may open up avenues for achieving a better understanding of the impact of the inclusion of MOF adsorbents on the mechanical properties of MMMs.
Controlling the size and morphology of ZIF NCs is important for optimizing their textural characteristics, including surface area and pore volume. To date, the control over the size of ZIF particles has been achieved mostly by directly modifying the synthetic parameters and to some degree, by the use of surfactants or non-ionic BCPs. In contrast, the shape control of ZIF NCs often relies heavily on template-mediated approaches rather than non-templated ones as a more diverse morphology of ZIF NCs can be achieved. By employing soft or hard templates, ZIF nanostructures with controlled morphologies such as nanotubes, spheres, cubes, and prisms have been successfully obtained. Although the shape control of ZIF NCs (and their corresponding derived materials) has been acquired to some degree, the exploration of recently emerging templates such as BCPs or inorganic metal acetate hydroxide may broaden the possibilities of morphology of ZIF particles which can be synthesized.
Over the past few years, there has been a rapidly growing interest in the use of ZIFs as templates for deriving nano­ porous carbon hybrid materials. This is due to their highly tunable composition, large surface area, controllable porosity and high carbon and nitrogen contents. In particular, the use of bimetallic ZIFs (e.g., ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67) as precursors to derive N, Co-doped porous carbons has gained momentum due to the possibility of combining the advantages of individual ZIF constituents, which may include higher graphitization degree, improved porosity, higher electrical conductivity and stability. Due to these advantages, future efforts should be focused more on the derivations of nanoporous hybrid materials from bimetallic ZIFs rather than from individual ZIFs, in order to optimize their functional properties. Despite the many advantages exhibited by ZIF-derived hybrids, it is still beneficial to combine them with other carbon-based materials such as N-doped reduced graphene oxide, CNTs or oxide-based materials to enhance their electrical conductivity for energy storage and conversion devices.
In terms of future applications, the superior chemical stability and vapor selective adsorption properties of ZIFs can be brought to a new height of technological development by developing ZIF-based 2D photonic structures and heterostructures as sensing platforms. Photonic ZIF patterns involving dynamic variation of optical properties depending on functional ZIFs as sensor matrix. The integration of functional ZIFs into 2D photonic components can pave the way for advanced optical devices in which the original properties of ZIFs (high porosity, sorption selectivity, etc.) and periodic structures (light confinement, diffraction, etc.) are combined and restored. For greater success with ZIF-derived materials, ZIFs need to be processed, shaped in nanoscale, and integrated in functional devices. This would especially enlarge the scope of applications for the utilization of ZIFs beyond the conventional regime.
